    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Research-Phlebotomists---Tackle-Concussion-Trial_R-65434-1": {
      "title": "Casual Research Phlebotomists – Tackle Concussion Trial",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nQueensland Brian Institute Multiple casual positions available at HEW Level 4 The hourly casual rate will range from $51.24 - $54.33 plus 12% Superannuation Administratively based at our St Lucia campus however travel will be required to schools and study sites across Brisbane About This Opportunity    We are seeking motivated and reliable Research Phlebotomists , with previous experience in the collection of bio-samples (blood, saliva, finger-prick), to support the UQ Concussion Study at our world-leading Queensland Brian Institute on a casual basis. The UQ Concussion Study is based on a partnership between World Rugby, Rugby Australia, the Great Public Schools Association (GPS) and focuses on developing a biomarker for concussion diagnosis and its recovery. The study will recruit participants from schools in South-East Queensland, and a multitude of data elements such as demographics, cognitive data, and bio-specimen samples, will be collected from participants at different timepoints. In this role, you will provide support to school staff and participants at participating schools. Key duties will include bio-sample collection including phlebotomy, record keeping, facilitating data collection processes at schools and providing support to the study sites when needed. These are casual positions and as such, work hours will vary from week to week depending on participant availability. Please note, due to the nature of the study, applicants will need to be able to work flexible out of hours and/or on Saturdays during the GPS rugby season (May to September). Applicants must also have access to their own reliable transport to travel to study sites. About You  Ideal candidates will be reliable, organised and able to work self-directed in a busy environment. You must be able to work with school staff and adolescent participants in a respectful and supportive manner, and you will be able to provide clear and easy-to-follow instructions regarding all study procedures to both school staff and participants/parents as required. In addition, you will possess: Certificate III in Pathology Collection (HLT37215) or an equivalent Australian recognised qualification. Previous training and experience in phlebotomy is essential. Conducting venepuncture on adolescents would be highly regarded. Training in all other study procedures will be provided. Excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to work autonomously i.e. flexibility to attend study sites at short notice, work autonomously and in small teams with a wide range of varied stakeholders. Strong organisational skills i.e. ability to keep organised records, and report self-directed, in timely manner. Proficient in the use of the Microsoft office suite products including Word, Excel and Outlook. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Fatima Nasrallah at f.nasrallah@uq.edu.au or Dr Alex Kanon at a.kanon@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number ( R-65434 ) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Other Information As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work, please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  Pre‑employment checks will include verification of the right to work in Australia (employer-sponsored work rights are not available for these appointments), mandatory immunisations and a Working with Children Blue Card. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.  We are dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ. We encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Application reference number: R-65434 Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early.   #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Casual-Associate-Animal-Technician_R-60694": {
      "title": "Casual Associate Animal Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-02-02\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nQueensland Animal Science Precinct (QASP) Join our casual pool for a range of associate animal technician opportunities   Competitive hourly rate of $44.67 , plus 11.5% superannuation Based at our Gatton Campus   About This Opportunity    We are seeking reliable and committed Associate Animal Technicians to join our casual pool, providing essential care and husbandry for research animals at the Queensland Animal Science Precinct (QASP). This role ensures the welfare of animals while supporting general facility operations, including the use of equipment, chemicals, and other materials. You'll contribute to maintenance tasks and the daily functionality of the QASP, working under routine or general supervision.    These casual roles are located at the Gatton Campus and may require some work outside of normal working hours, including participation on the weekend/public holiday roster.    Duties and Responsibilities Include:  Animal Husbandry   Provide care and welfare for livestock, ensuring clean housing and maintaining biosecurity.  Perform record-keeping and assist with research sampling and euthanasia as needed.  Operational Support   Prepare facilities, operate machinery, and ensure compliance with safety and regulatory standards.  Support daily operations, including waste management and effective team communication.  Research Support   Assist in research projects by performing routine procedures and providing experimental support.  Participate in rosters, including weekend/public holiday duties, as required.  About UQ's Gatton Campus Established over 125 years ago, UQ’s Gatton Campus has a long history – from starting out as a dedicated agricultural institution, to being a field hospital during WWII, and to joining the UQ community in 1990.     Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our casual staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.      Join an organisation where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and intercampus bus    On campus childcare , medical clinic, and post office   Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sports facility    Volunteer in our community garden    About You    Essential:   Certificate III in Agriculture (or equivalent) and relevant work experience.  Possession of a C Class Licence and at least 2 years’ experience in livestock handling and animal husbandry.  Competence in livestock techniques (e.g., SC/IM injections, blood collection).  Experience operating plant and machinery (e.g., tractors, forklifts) and capacity to obtain required licences/permits.  Ability to safely operate and use hand tools and basic equipment required for animal husbandry, facility maintenance support, and routine technical tasks, in accordance with OH&S standards. Strong attention to detail, ability to work independently, and excellent team collaboration and communication skills.  Desirable:   Knowledge of OH&S, OGTR, DAWE regulatory requirements, and animal welfare codes.  Experience in a research environment or meat processing. The successful candidate may be required to complete a number of pre-employment checks, including: right to work in Australia and Medical Clearance.  You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     Medical Clearance: Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position.    Vaccinations and Immunisation: It is a condition of employment for this role that if you are required now or in the future, to work or interact in Queensland Health clinical facility; or in an equivalent, clinical health facility; or health care role; or will be required to perform work tasks that put you at risk of exposure to vaccine-preventable disease you are required to be immunised against, and remain immunised against, certain vaccine-preventable diseases (VPDs) in accordance with the University’s Vaccinations and Immunisation Guidelines (PPL 2.60.08). The employee is required to provide evidence of immunisation against VPDs.  Questions?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     Want to Apply?    All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered:  Cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section   Resume   Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.   We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au for accessibility support or adjustments.  Applications for this casual pool will be reviewed on an ongoing basis (R-60694). #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Dental-Assistants_R-52986": {
      "title": "Casual Dental Assistants",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-01-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nSchool of Dentistry Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $43.79 to $48.20, plus superannuation of 11.5% (HEW Level 3) Based at the Oral Health Centre, Herston campus About This Opportunity The University of Queensland's School of Dentistry is inviting expressions of interest from qualified dental assistants who are interested in casual opportunities. As a Casual Dental Assistant, you will support students and UQ clinical staff at the School of Dentistry’s student-led Dental Clinic. Your responsibilities will include (but are not limited to): Supporting clinical operations to ensure a smooth, effective teaching and treatment environment. Set up, clean, sterilise, and store dental instruments and equipment in line with infection control standards. Maintain accurate clinic records and uphold professional presentation standards. Book, receive and prepare patients for treatment under the supervision of the Clinical Coordinator. About You We welcome applications from Dental Assistants who hold a Certificate III in Dental Assisting (or equivalent Dental Assisting Qualification) and have hands-on experience as a chairside Dental Assistant. A Certificate IV in Dental Assisting (Radiography stream) would be desirable. Experience with Chairside Assisting Minimum of 1–2 years’ experience assisting during general and/or specialty dental procedures, with strong skills in instrument handling, sterilisation, and patient preparation Excellent Interpersonal and Communication Skills Demonstrated ability to communicate clearly and compassionately with patients and team members Willingness to Learn and Grow Professionally Demonstrates a proactive attitude toward learning new skills, techniques, and technologies in a dynamic clinical environment. Open to ongoing training and development opportunities. The successful candidate may be required to complete background checks prior to commencement including the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations, and Blue Card check. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world. Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.  Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Anna Barrett at a.barrett@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    Want to Apply? We welcome applications from all individuals and are committed to an inclusive and accessible recruitment process. To be considered, please ensure you upload: Resume  Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact talent@uq.edu.au . Other Information   As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role. Application reference number R-52986. Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Dentistry_R-59295": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Dentistry",
      "description": "Start date: 2025-12-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity  The University of Queensland School of Dentistry is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics to be engaged on a casual basis. Casual Academics at the School of Dentistry play a significant part in the overall student experience and assist in the teaching and delivery of both undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Preparing and delivering guest lecturers, workshops, seminars and tutorials. Undertaking marking of assessment tasks, as directed by course coordinators, with hours for marking to be confirmed by School Administration staff prior to commencing marking. Casual academic positions are based at both the Herston and St Lucia Campuses and successful appointees may be required to attend on campus. Our Ideal Candidate  The School welcomes applications from candidates who possess appropriate qualifications and experience within the dental industry. High-achieving postgraduate coursework and research higher degree students are also invited to apply. What We Can Offer  These are casual positions. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed here . Compliance  All Casual Academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.  About the School of Dentistry The UQ School of Dentistry has a long and proud tradition of over 80 years of strength in dental education and research and attracts high achieving students nationally and internationally. UQ is a leading source of expertise in the local, national, and international dental community and has been a key contributor to achieving effective solutions to contemporary issues and challenges. The School has strong collaborations within and outside the dental profession. As part of the Faculty of Health, Medicine and Behavioural Sciences, the School of Dentistry offers the Bachelor of Dental Science (Honours) program and specialist post-graduate programs Doctor of Clinical Dentistry and the Doctor of Dental Medicine. The School of Dentistry also has a growing cohort of research higher degree students. Further details about the School’s teaching programs, research interests and other activities may be accessed on the School’s website at http://www.dentistry.uq.edu.au . Questions?  If you have any questions regarding this expression of interest, please contact the School of Dentistry, Acting Student and Academic Administration Manager, Mrs Anna Jackson at anna.jackson@uq.edu.au . Want To Apply?     All applicants must provide a resume to apply.  The School of Dentistry employs Casual Academics as required throughout the year. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Learning-Designer--Career-Development_R-65786-2": {
      "title": "Educational Content Developer (Learning Design) - Identified (s25)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCareer Development This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement as the role requires applying Indigenous perspectives and providing culturally informed advice to deliver inclusive career development programs. Full-time, Fixed-term position for up to 3 years  with Base salary in the range of $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)- Based at our  Vibrant St Lucia Campus. Acknowledgement of Country    The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.   About This Opportunity    Help shape culturally responsive educational content and learning experiences that support student success, employability and inclusion. As the Educational Content Developer (Learning Design), you'll collaborate with Indigenous communities, students and UQ stakeholders to reimagine and elevate career development programs.  resources and initiatives that embed Indigenous perspectives and ways of knowing, being and doing. This is an opportunity to make a meaningful impact through work that advances reconciliation, promotes cultural inclusion, and strengthens educational and career outcomes for students across UQ. This important work will  driv e  real impact for students and shap e  more inclusive outcomes. This is your chance to make a lasting difference, collaborating across diverse stakeholders to deliver better futures for students and communities. Key Responsibilities Will include: Develop engaging educational content, learning resources and digital materials that support student employability and career development. Collaborate with academic staff, professional teams and Indigenous stakeholders to create inclusive and culturally responsive learning experiences. Embed Indigenous perspectives, knowledges and pedagogies into career development initiatives and educational resources. Provide advice and guidance on culturally responsive approaches to learning, student engagement and employability. Evaluate programs and resources, using feedback and insights to drive continuous improvement and enhance student outcomes. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet .   About You    The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence confirming that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. You will also bring: Completion of an undergraduate degree, with three to four years’ experience in a relevant area or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. Experience developing digital educational content, learning resources, programs or initiatives that support diverse learners. Demonstrated ability to embed Indigenous perspectives, knowledges and culturally responsive practices in educational, community or engagement settings. Experience working with educational technologies and digital learning platforms such as Learning Management Systems and online content tools. Strong communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work collaboratively with and influence a wide range of stakeholders across diverse teams. The ability to manage competing priorities, projects, analyse data and information and contribute to continuous improvement initiatives. An understanding of higher education and student employability initiatives (desirable).  At UQ, we value diverse experiences and perspectives. We understand that no candidate is likely to tick every box, and we're interested in hearing from people who can bring their unique skills, lived experience, knowledge and passion to the role. If you feel you can contribute to our work and help advance positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities, we encourage you to apply. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Aaron Ruutz on a.ruutz@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 4 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R- 65786).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Week commencing 12th October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Heron-Island-Research-Station/Research-Station-Support-Services-Assistant--Heron-Island----Identified--s25-_R-62194-3": {
      "title": "Research Station Support Services Assistant (Heron Island) - Identified (s25)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science 2 x Full time permanent positions Base salary will be in the range $79,096.42- $83,863.22 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 4)  This position is open to Traditional Owners of Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng or Taribelang Bunda Peoples – the Traditional Custodians of the land and sea Country encompassing Heron Island and surrounding areas. Based at Heron Island Research Station Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity    Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team at UQ’s Heron Island Research Station, where scientific excellence meets cultural stewardship on Country. This identified role supports the day-to-day operations of UQ’s research station while embedding Traditional Owner knowledge, values, and perspectives into environmental management and research activities. We are seeking two positions with complementary focus areas: One position with a stronger emphasis on maintenance and facility operations . One position with a stronger emphasis on administration, outreach, and research support services . Working as part of a collaborative team, both Research Station Support Services Assistant roles contribute to the overall daily operations of UQ’s research stations. This work may be supporting the delivery of specific services including scientific (e.g. laboratory and aquaria), boating, snorkelling and diving, administrative, general maintenance and other operational tasks and activities required to maintain the safe and effective functioning of the research station. Key responsibilities include: Assisting with operational services including administration, logistics, and facility upkeep across the station Supporting the preparation, use, and maintenance of scientific, boating, and diving equipment to enable research and teaching activities Contributing to culturally informed environmental management practices and promoting awareness of cultural heritage protocols Collaborating with staff, researchers, and visitors to ensure safe, efficient, and respectful station operations On-site accommodation will be provided by UQ while undertaking work on Heron Island . Take our virtual tour . For further information, please  click here  to view the information booklet.  About You    The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the  Anti-Discrimination Act 1991  (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  Must be a Traditional Owner of the Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng, or Taribelang Bunda Peoples – the Traditional Custodians of the land and sea Country encompassing Heron Island and surrounding areas. Connection to and knowledge of the cultural heritage, traditions, and values of the Traditional Owners of Heron Island and surrounding areas. Vocational training qualification (or ready ability to acquire) in science, business, or a trade with relevant experience, OR an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education, and training. Demonstrated ability to follow complex processes and systems, ideally where necessary for health and safety, animal ethics, and/or environmental compliance. Current holder of (or ready ability to attain and maintain) the necessary qualifications and experience for formal recognition in the operation of plant and heavy machinery. High level computer literacy skills including the ability to use, or learn to use, software applications associated with analytical instruments and business operations. Demonstrated high level of interpersonal skills, with a proven ability to develop and sustain strategic relationships, to work collaboratively and undertake effective consultations with client groups. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr  Caitlin Lawson at  caitlin.lawson@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 21 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-62194).   Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late September/early October."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Program-Officer--Widening-Participation--Identified-s105-_R-68973-2": {
      "title": "Program Officer, Widening Participation (Identified s105)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nOutreach Program Coordination team (SES) This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, reflecting UQ's commitment to increasing Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representation and fostering a diverse, inclusive and culturally rich workforce. Full-time, permanent with base salary in the range of $85,451 - $94,983 + 17% super (HEW Level 5)  Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus. Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity    Join UQ as a Program Officer and help inspire the next generation of students by creating inclusive and engaging experiences that open pathways to higher education. In this varied and rewarding role, you will coordinate outreach programs, workshops and events, support UQ Outreach Ambassadors and Mentors, and contribute to the successful delivery of initiatives that connect students with university life. Working closely with partner schools, students, UQ colleagues and external stakeholders, you'll help deliver meaningful experiences for students from a broad range of backgrounds, including communities historically underrepresented in higher education. Your strong organisational skills and attention to detail will help ensure programs are delivered smoothly, efficiently and with a positive impact. Key responsibilities will include: Support the administration, logistics and delivery of outreach programs, events and educational activities. Develop and maintain positive relationships with partner schools and key internal and external stakeholders. Support the successful delivery of the Ambassador and Mentor Program through planning, scheduling, communication and administration. Maintain program records, resources, budgets and reporting to ensure effective program delivery and continuous improvement. Bring flexibility to support key outreach activities and events, which may occasionally involve early starts from 7am or evening finishes up to 8pm , while working standard daily hours. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet .   About You     The University of Queensland considers that the filling of this position is intended to constitute a special / equal opportunity measure under section 105 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld) and section 8(1) of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth).  The position is therefore only open to Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander applicants. The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  You will bring a service-focused and highly organised approach, along with the confidence to coordinate many moving parts and communicate with a diverse range of stakeholders. You will also demonstrate:   Relevant education or equivalent experience in student services, outreach, community engagement, program administration or a related area. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with careful attention to detail when preparing correspondence, checking bookings and confirming logistics. Demonstrated administration and event coordination capability, including confidence using digital systems and the ability to quickly learn specialist platforms. The ability to prioritise, multitask and meet deadlines in a busy environment while maintaining accurate records and dependable follow-through. An understanding of the regional, social, cultural and financial barriers that can affect participation in higher education, particularly for students from underrepresented backgrounds. A current unrestricted Queensland driver’s licence , as occasional travel to partner schools and community locations is required. At UQ, we value diverse experiences and perspectives. We understand that no candidate is likely to tick every box, and we are interested in hearing from people who can bring their unique skills, lived experience, knowledge and passion to the role. If you are passionate about helping students from diverse and underrepresented backgrounds explore pathways to higher education and contributing to positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities, we would love to hear from you. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebecca Dwan on r.dwan@uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, Blue Card checks and First Aid and CPR Certification. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Monday 5 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R- 68973). Applications will be assessed on a rolling basis during the advertising period. If your skills and experience align strongly with the role, you may be invited to interview prior to the closing date."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Project-Officer_R-69087": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nSchool of Political Science & International Studies Part-time (40%), fixed-term until 31 May 2028 Full-time equivalent base salary will be in the range $97,268 to $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Based at our vibrant and picturesque St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join the School of Political Science and International Studies as a Senior Project Officer and play a key role in delivering an exciting ARC Future Fellowship project, “Paths to Primacy: How Rising Powers Win Domination in Asia, 1500–Present.” Working closely with Professor Andrew Phillips and School leadership, you will support the end-to-end coordination and delivery of this significant research project. You’ll coordinate public-facing events, engage with high-level stakeholders, support research dissemination, and manage project timelines, budgets, travel and communications. This is an excellent opportunity for a highly motivated, experienced and versatile project professional to contribute to a high-profile research initiative, working across a diverse range of stakeholders and activities to deliver strategic and operational outcomes. Key responsibilities will include, but are not limited to :  Coordinate the project delivery cycle, including planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation, while tracking milestones, timelines and deliverables. Manage and deliver scholarly workshops, public-facing events and stakeholder meetings, including event design, invitations, logistics, communications and follow-up. Promote project outputs through online, social media and traditional platforms, and support the analysis and communication of project impact. Monitor project expenditure and financial commitments, maintain accurate working budget records, and support forecasting, administration and reporting. About You   A Bachelor’s degree and/or relevant experience in the tertiary education, government or industry sector, combined with demonstrated project management capability. Demonstrated experience coordinating complex projects involving multiple deliverables, deadlines and stakeholders. A developing understanding of project management methodologies and frameworks, with the ability to support planning, monitoring, evaluation and reporting. Excellent organisational and time-management skills, including the ability to balance competing priorities and deliver outcomes within tight timeframes. Advanced written and oral communication skills, with the ability to prepare clear project documentation, updates, briefs and research dissemination materials. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively and exercise diplomacy and judgement when engaging with senior academic and professional staff, government and non-government representatives, industry partners, donors, alumni and project participants. Experience using Microsoft tools, social media and web content platforms, or a demonstrated ability to learn new project management and communication systems quickly. An interest in the social sciences, particularly political science and international studies, is welcomed but not required. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Katrina Lee Koo, Head of School at k.leekoo@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 28 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69087). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Monday 12 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer---How-Rising-Powers-Win-Domination-in-Asia--1500-Present_R-69104-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer - How Rising Powers Win Domination in Asia, 1500–Present",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nSchool of Political Science & International Studies Part-time (70%), fixed-term position until 31 May 2028 Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our vibrant and picturesque St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer to develop your expertise and emerging research profile while contributing to the ARC Future Fellowship project, Paths to Primacy: How Rising Powers Win Domination in Asia, 1500–Present . Working closely with Professor Andrew Phillips in the School of Political Science and International Studies, you will join a collaborative and intellectually stimulating research environment exploring how rising powers have sought, achieved and maintained regional primacy across different historical periods and parts of Asia, and what these experiences mean for the contemporary evolution of international order. Key responsibilities will include, but are not limited to:  Research: Undertake high-quality independent and collaborative research aligned with the Future Fellowship project, producing peer-reviewed publications and other scholarly and public-facing outputs, while developing a complementary research program, contributing to workshops, conferences and research meetings, and building purposeful networks with researchers, policy practitioners and external stakeholders. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, as appropriate to the level of appointment. Citizenship and Service: Collaborate in School activities, support academic and operational priorities, demonstrate UQ values, and contribute to engagement with government, industry, professional bodies and the wider community. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  Completion or near-completion of a PhD in International Relations. A research agenda demonstrably connected to rising powers, international order, Asian International Relations or a closely related field. Demonstrated capacity to undertake theoretically informed comparative or historical research. Evidence of research publications in reputable peer-reviewed academic outlets. Capacity to work both independently and collaboratively, including translating agreed project objectives into completed research outputs. Excellent research organisation, source-management and written and verbal communication skills. Expertise concerning one or more Asian regions or historical periods relevant to the project. Asian-language capability that supports the proposed research contribution, or experience communicating research to government, policy or public audiences, would be highly regarded. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Andrew Phillips at andrew.phillips@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday, 28 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69104). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Monday, 12 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Customs-House/Casual-Food-and-Beverage-Attendants_R-68808-1": {
      "title": "Casual Food and Beverage Attendants",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nCasual\n\nCustoms House – 399 Queen Street Award-winnin g venue – Best Wedding Caterer of QLD and NT on the Restaurant & Catering Awards for Excellence 2026 $39.12 per hour + 12% superannuation contributions Based at the heart of Brisbane City About This Opportunity  Bring memorable events and dining experiences to life at Customs House, one of Brisbane’s distinctive heritage hospitality venues. We are seeking energetic, polished and customer-focused Food and Beverage Attendants to join our collaborative team across Banquets Operations and Patina Restaurant. You will work in an inclusive, high-performing environment where professional service, teamwork and attention to detail create exceptional experiences for every guest. Staff must be available to work across a seven-day roster, including early mornings, evenings, late nights and weekends. Key responsibilities will include: Deliver professional food and beverage service and create a warm, memorable experience for guests. Set up and pack down restaurant, function and bar spaces, including equipment and furniture. Provide plate, clearing and tray service across banquets, events and restaurant operations. Prepare and serve beverages, including mixed drinks and cocktails, while applying sound wine knowledge. Support stock control, storage, presentation and the cleanliness of front-of-house and back-of-house areas. Communicate clearly during service and contribute to smooth handovers and collaborative teamwork. About You You will bring: A current Queensland Responsible Service of Alcohol certificate. Approximately two years of experience in a comparable food and beverage, restaurant, events or premium hospitality role. A genuine commitment to creating positive guest experiences through attentive and professional service. Experience with plate carrying, plate clearing and tray service. Confidence serving beverages, with cocktail-making skills and wine knowledge highly regarded. A positive, dependable and customer-focused approach. Strong communication and teamwork skills, with the ability to contribute during busy service periods. Flexibility to work across a seven-day roster, including early mornings, evenings, late nights and weekends. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a purposeful and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies. At Customs House, you will be part of a supportive team that values your hospitality experience and offers opportunities to learn, connect and grow. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ms Carolina Muramoto, Banquet/Event Manager, at c.muramoto@customshouse.com.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload: A resume. Evidence of your current Queensland Responsible Service of Alcohol certificate. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position. Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. People with criminal records are not automatically barred from applying, and each application will be considered on its merits. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Please let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. If you are a current UQ employee, including a casual staff member or HDR scholar, or hold an unpaid or affiliate appointment, please sign in to your staff Workday account and apply through the internal careers board. Please do not apply through the external job board. Applications close Wednesday 14 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-66808). Applications will be reviewed as received, and candidates may be interviewed before the closing date. We encourage you to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close the application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Output-and-Impact-Specialist_R-68584-1": {
      "title": "Research Output and Impact Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nResearch Outputs & Impact team (UQ Library) Turn research metrics, bibliometrics and altmetrics data into meaningful insights that shape research strategy and decision-making. Full-time, Permanent with Base salary in the range of $106,709 - $116,198.52   + 17% super (HEW Level 7)  Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus. About This Opportunity    Join a role where data, research, and stakeholder engagement come together to make a meaningful impact. The UQ Library is seeking a Research Output and Impact Specialist to help researchers understand, evidence, and communicate the reach and influence of their work. Collaborating with teams in the Library, Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Research and Innovation), faculties and research institutes, you'll provide expert advice, training and reporting services that support research planning and evaluation, and informed decision-making across the University. Key responsibilities will include:    Provide leadership, training and support to Library staff and others in the University on research metrics, bibliometrics, altmetrics, and research evaluation. In close collaboration with the Faculty Services Librarian team, initiate and maintain relationships with individual researchers and/or research units to promote and enhance research output and impact services. Deliver high-quality reports, training resources, and recommendations to support researchers and research units applying for grant applications, and with research planning and evaluation activities.   Develop and maintain relationships with research service stakeholders internal and external to UQ. Contribut to University and Library projects that strengthen researcher and staff capability in metrics, engagement and impact services Provide advice, recommendations and support to the Manager, Research Outputs and Impact, and deputise for the Manager as required. Actively participate in University or Government research evaluation programs. Contribute as an effective Library team member including leading and/or participating in planning activities and the delivery of team and broader Library goals. About You     Our ideal candidate will have: A University degree with substantial experience in a University Library or research support environment, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Substantial knowledge and experience providing high level services to support and enhance activities relating to research evaluation, research outputs, and bibliometrics with confidence using relevant reporting tools and current technologies. In depth knowledge and awareness of the research environment and current issues in Australian higher education relating to research, and an understanding and awareness of the role and potential role of a University Library in supporting its institutional activities. Excellent communication skills, with demonstrated emotional intelligence and the ability to negotiate, consult and effectively build relationships to engage diverse groups including library staff, researchers, professional staff and external stakeholders.   Experience working in environments requiring a high level of accuracy and attention to detail, including written reports and presentations. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, including the ability to manipulate numeric data, statistics and bibliometric indicators.  Drive and enthusiasm with a strong work ethic and willingness to go the extra mile to accomplish tasks in a fast-paced environment. Excellent team player working collaboratively with others, with a positive and cooperative attitude, and a mindset of inclusivity and support for colleagues.  Excellent organisation skills with demonstrated ability to manage multiple tasks or projects effectively against differing timelines or changing priorities. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebecca Deuble on r.deuble@library.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia or criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Friday 25 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68584). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 15th and 16th of October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Fellow-in-Statistical-Genomics_R-68861-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Fellow in Statistical Genomics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nInstitute for Molecular Bioscience Full-time fixed-term position for up to 2.5 years Base salary will be in the range $106,293.57 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) or $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative statistical genomics research environment led by Professor Loic Yengo. This opportunity is designed for an ambitious researcher seeking to establish an independent research program and transition towards leading their own laboratory within the next five years. Working within a supportive and inclusive team, you will contribute to globally relevant research that advances understanding of complex traits and diseases through innovative statistical genetics methodologies, while helping create meaningful change through discovery, translation and impact. This position is being offered at either Academic Level A or Academic Level B, depending on the successful candidate's experience, track record and demonstrated research leadership. We welcome applications from outstanding recent PhD graduates as well as candidates with postdoctoral experience who are seeking to establish or further develop an independent research profile. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Produce high-quality research outputs including peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, software tools, and competitive research funding applications, while contributing to the translation and impact of research outcomes. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and mentoring of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, while fostering capability development and supporting a positive research culture. Citizenship and Service: Develop collaborative partnerships, contribute to internal service activities and committees, support colleagues, and uphold University values through leadership, professionalism and engagement. Additional for level B: Research: Lead or contribute to the development of innovative statistical methods for analysing large-scale genomic and single-cell omics datasets, identifying causal genetic variation, and improving phenotype prediction and gene prioritisation across genetically diverse populations. Funding: Lead competitive research funding applications such as research grants and fellowships. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  You will demonstrate some or all of the following: A PhD in Statistical Genetics, Quantitative Genetics, Computational Biology, Statistics, Data Science or a closely related discipline. Knowledge of genetics and genomics and a passion for applying quantitative approaches to biological and medical research questions. Strong expertise in (Bayesian) statistics and data science Experience with mathematical modelling (e.g., linear algebra, linear models methodologies) Demonstrated programming skills in C/C++, Python, R or comparable languages. Experience with AI-based coding tools such as Claude Code, Codex or Cursor. Evidence of research productivity through publications, presentations or research outputs appropriate to career stage. Excellent communication, collaboration, organisational and problem-solving skills. The ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a multidisciplinary research environment. Experience with quantitative genetics, Bayesian methods, machine learning, large-scale genomic datasets, single-cell omics or integrative omics analyses would be highly regarded if the candidate was not initially trained in those disciplines. For appointment at Academic Level B , candidates will additionally demonstrate: A developing track record of research leadership and increasing national or international recognition. Experience contributing to or leading competitive research funding applications. Experience supervising or mentoring research students and/or junior researchers. Evidence of establishing collaborative research partnerships and contributing to broader research impact. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Loic Yengo at l.yengo@imb.uq.edu.au. For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 12 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68861)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Librarian-Archivist_R-68683": {
      "title": "Librarian/Archivist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUQ Library Collecting, arranging and describing nationally significant collections in Australian literature, history and architecture.  2 x full-time, fixed-term opportunities with Base salary in the range of $97,268-$104,348, + generous 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity We have an exciting opportunity for two experienced Librarian/Archivists to contribute to the development, stewardship and discovery of the Fryer Library’s special collections through collection development, archival description and metadata creation. One opportunity is for a period of 3 months (backfilling Long Service Leave 28 October 2026 – 29 January 2027). The second opportunity is for a period of 12 months (backfilling Parental Leave) from November 2026. Reporting to the Senior Librarian, Collections, you will be responsible for collection development and management activities including the arrangement and description of nationally significant manuscript collections in Australian literature and Queensland architecture, Aboriginal and Torres Strait islander languages and culture, and the identification and acquisition of published works in line with our Collection Development Policy. As Fryer Library prepares to celebrate its Centenary in 2027, we are continuing to improve discovery of our special collections while exploring innovative ways to broaden their reach and impact through metadata, digital technologies and enhanced online access. Key responsibilities will include: Arrange and describe manuscript collections at an advanced level, using established archival procedures and standards, as well as internal policies and guidelines to support inclusive and reparative description. Develop the Fryer Library’s special collections in accordance with collection development policies and guidelines, to enhance the teaching, learning and research objectives of The University. Interpret and administer complex copyright, licensing and access protocols, including Indigenous Cultural Intellectual Property (ICIP), liaising with the Senior Librarian (Collections), the Copyright Officer, and/or Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Services and Collections team, as required. Mentor and support the work of other library staff as required, including training. Build effective relationships with colleagues, clients and donors through high-level communication (including negotiation), interpersonal client service, and teamwork skills. Please note this role is based primarily on campus due to the nature of duties and responsibilities involved, but flexible arrangements can be negotiated. About You You’ll be an experienced, flexible, and tech-savvy information professional with special collections experience, ideally in an academic context.   Essential: A degree accredited by the Australian Library and Information Association or the Australian Society of Archivists with subsequent relevant experience, or extensive expertise in special collections, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education and/or training. Demonstrated experience interpreting and applying standards to the arrangement and description of manuscripts, using relevant systems and technologies for the management and discovery of special collections. Experience interpreting and applying complex copyright, licensing, and access protocols for special collections, including communicating requirements to donors and clients. Demonstrated interpersonal, communication, collaboration, and time management skills with the ability to organise and prioritise own workloads, work independently, meet deadlines, and lead and motivate others to achieve team goals. Desirable: Experience in working with collections that contain Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander languages and culture, and a working knowledge of ICIP will be highly regarded. Experience or interest in working with born digital materials will be highly regarded. Experience and/or familiarity of the rare book market, and application of that knowledge to the development of the library’s special collections.  Experience in the use of Library Management Systems and/or Archival Management Systems such as Alma/Primo and AtoM, or other similar systems. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging About Fryer Library Founded in 1927, the Fryer Library’s collections include Australian literary publications, manuscripts, photographs, architectural drawings, audiovisual recordings, and rare books. Part of the University of Queensland (UQ) Library, the Fryer Library plays a critical role supporting research, UQ teaching and community engagement. Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Serena Coates on s.coates@library.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history check. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.    You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Tuesday 22 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68683) - Please note that onsite interviews are tentatively scheduled for week commencing 28 September 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Teaching-Focused-Lecturer-Senior-Lecturer-in-Data-Science-and-Artificial-Intelligence_R-67597": {
      "title": "Teaching Focused Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Data Science and Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time permanent position Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) or $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative team on a mission to create the most sought-after postgraduate artificial intelligence degrees in Australia. The School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science is seeking to appoint a Teaching Focused academic at Lecturer (Level B) or Senior Lecturer (Level C) level with expertise in artificial intelligence and/or data science. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to the development of a flagship suite of postgraduate AI programs that challenge traditional approaches to university assessment and place authentic, competency-based learning at the centre of the student experience. Working within a diverse and supportive academic community, you will help shape innovative programs while contributing to education-focused scholarship, student success, and the University’s broader mission of creating meaningful change through teaching, learning, and research. Key responsibilities will include:  Teaching: Develop and deliver new courses within postgraduate artificial intelligence programs, lead curriculum and course design activities, coordinate courses, assess student learning, provide academic advice and support, and collaborate with learning designers to create authentic, competency-based assessment models that reflect contemporary professional practice. Supervision and Researcher Development: Undertake high-quality research in AI and/or data science, contribute to competitive funding applications, supervise coursework thesis projects, and co-supervise research higher degree students where appropriate. Citizenship and Service: Develop partnerships with industry, government, and academic collaborators, demonstrate leadership through mentoring and collegial support, contribute to School and University committees, undertake administrative responsibilities, and uphold UQ’s values of excellence, integrity, respect, and inclusivity. This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  You are a passionate educator and scholar who is committed to developing exceptional learning experiences and preparing graduates to thrive in a rapidly evolving technological landscape. You will demonstrate the following: A PhD in artificial intelligence, data science, computer science, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in artificial intelligence and/or data science, with the ability to teach across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. An emerging profile of teaching excellence and/or education-focused scholarship in artificial intelligence or data science, demonstrated through curriculum innovation, scholarly outputs, teaching awards, educational leadership, teaching recognition and a publicly accessible Google Scholar profile (please include a link in your application). Demonstrated experience applying contemporary pedagogical approaches to design, develop and evaluate engaging, student-centred learning experiences. Experience designing authentic assessments and competency-based evaluation frameworks that align with professional practice. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively across academic, professional and industry communities. Evidence of, or capacity for, contributing to education-focused scholarship through publications, presentations, or other impactful scholarly outcomes. For appointment at Level C, evidence of a growing national or international profile in education-focused scholarship within artificial intelligence or data science, demonstrated through impactful scholarly outputs in high-quality journals and conferences, successful supervision of Honours, Masters, and/or Research Higher Degree students to completion, and evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing external research funding. We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Tim Miller at Timothy.miller@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section that includes a link to your public Google Scholar profile . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 12 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67597)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Librarian_R-69004-1": {
      "title": "Librarian",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nLibrary (Faculty Services). Full-time, permanent with Base salary in the range of $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) - Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus. About This Opportunity    We are seeking a Librarian to support the effective delivery of client-focused Library services in the Faculty Services Librarian team. The role is responsible for leading the delivery of a wide range of proactive, discipline-based scholarly information and communication services to clients, while ensuring the Library remains responsive and contributes to UQ’s learning, discovery and engagement goals.   Key Responsibilities Will include: Scholarly Information Services Provide complex discipline specific proactive scholarly information services, including: library and information services for clients; review and analysis of publishing trends and research impact reporting; and review of emergent scholarly information resources, tools and services. Develop and maintain strong and effective partnerships with UQ academic staff and students to maximise the value of library expertise and service in enhancing UQ’s teaching and learning. Engage with academic staff and researchers to maintain a strong understanding of their research, scholarship and teaching to ensure effective promotion of Library services. Client Support Contribute to the provision of high-quality services to all clients across the Library. Support individual researchers and research teams by providing advice related to research data management, and by providing bibliometrics to support grant applications. Design, deliver and evaluate information literacy programs as part of a cross-campus team in a flexible learning environment. Contribute, document and maintain accurate records, statistics and data entries in Library Client Relationship Management systems. Collection Development Develop the Library’s collections in collaboration with academics, researchers and other Library staff in context of the Library’s Collection Management Policy to enhance the teaching, learning and research objectives of The University. Library Operations Develop, publish and maintain library documentation including online content. Coordinate and participate in University Committees and Library projects as required. May mentor and/or supervise the work of other library staff in designated areas. Other duties as reasonably directed by the supervisor. About You Our ideal candidate will hold: Relevant qualifications which qualify for associate membership of the Australian Library and Information Association. Professional experience in the information services area of an academic, special or research library. Knowledge of current trends in research, learning and teaching within an academic institution. Substantial knowledge of information resources, services and publishing trends in academic libraries. Highly developed skills in the use of current tools and technologies for the delivery of information services. Ability to provide advanced information assistance and information literacy programs through a variety of flexible delivery options. Subject expertise relevant to the learning and research needs with relevant liaison experience in an academic community. Understanding of research output and metrics, including citation patterns. Excellent communication, liaison interpersonal, customer service and teamwork skills. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Maria Larkin on m.larkin@library.uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information     P re-employment checks may include : verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks . This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.       You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.      We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Friday 25 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69004)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Business-Analyst---Data-Governance_R-65385-1": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst - Data Governance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nInformation Technology Services Full-time (100%), permanent position Base salary will be in the range $117,958.12 – $132,247.57 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our St Lucia campus About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and forward-thinking team where your expertise will help shape the University’s data governance capability. As a Senior Business Analyst within Data Strategy and Governance, you will play a critical role in enabling strong data practices, working with the Solution Architect and diverse stakeholders to develop UQ's conceptual data models and key data governance artefacts. As a Senior Business Analyst, you will have the opportunity to showcase your expertise in identifying and defining business requirements and risks, providing data governance advice and representing business processes and information in models of varying complexity. Key responsibilities will include: Partner with stakeholders across the University to elicit, analyse and define risks and business requirements, providing complex data governance advice, direction or recommendations Prepare key documentation to support business analysis, such as business cases, risk reviews, conceptual data models and issue papers Develop and maintain enterprise data artefacts such as the information entity catalogue and business glossary Facilitate workshops with stakeholders and work with the Solution Architect to develop conceptual data models, define key information entities and business terms, and help identify data-related issues Support the Solution Architect to develop and implement UQ's data architecture framework Drive the successful delivery of data initiatives by managing changes to conceptual data models, business glossary definitions and supporting the development of more detailed models where needed Supervise the Data Governance Officer to meet their operational objectives, ensuring all aspects of human resource management are conducted effectively in accordance with University policies and procedures About You Postgraduate qualifications in Information Technology or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience Demonstrated significant experience in business analysis, including requirements elicitation, risk management, analysis, and documentation Proven experience in data governance, information management, enterprise architecture, or data services environments Demonstrated ability to develop business process models, conceptual data models or other enterprise architecture Exceptional analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to interpret complex information and develop practical solutions Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate, and collaborate with diverse stakeholders Strong written communication skills with experience producing high-quality reports and artefacts Ability to work independently, manage competing priorities, and deliver outcomes within agreed timeframes Desirable qualifications include : Experience working in a regulated environment with knowledge of Queensland's regulatory environment regarding data, information and records Experience modelling using the ArchiMate modelling language and using architecture or modelling tools (E.g. Avolution, Lean IX, Visio, Erwin) Experience working in the higher education sector or in a large IT department Knowledge of information technology services provided in a large organisation About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ellee McClymont, Senior Manager, Data Strategy & Governance at e.mcclymont@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 24 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-65385)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Sustainability-Officer_R-68824": {
      "title": "Principal Sustainability Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nProperty and Facilities Division Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,199 plus 17% super (HEW Level 7). Based at our stunning St Lucia campus . About This Opportunity Turn sustainability ambition into action across one of Australia’s leading universities. UQ is looking for a Principal Sustainability Officer to help shape and deliver the next generation of operational sustainability initiatives across UQ. This is a hands-on, high-impact role for someone who wants to do more than write sustainability strategies. You’ll have the opportunity to identify what is possible, build the case for change and lead initiatives through from idea to implementation, creating measurable environmental outcomes across a large and diverse organisation. Working within UQ’s Energy & Sustainability team and reporting to the Senior Building Services Engineer, you’ll provide specialist sustainability expertise across areas including biodiversity, water, waste, environmental performance and behaviour change . With UQ’s Sustainability Framework and Plan 2026–2029 providing a renewed roadmap for action, you’ll join at an exciting time to help translate our sustainability commitments into practical projects across our campuses and operations. Key responsibilities will include: Support sustainability projects from concept to delivery , with a focus on biodiversity, water management, waste reduction, environmental performance and behaviour change. Scanning the horizon for new ideas, emerging technologies and innovative approaches that could improve UQ’s environmental performance. Turning opportunities into action by developing compelling business cases, grant applications, project proposals and scopes to secure investment and funding. Building partnerships across UQ and with government, industry and external organisations to create shared sustainability outcomes. Coordinating multidisciplinary teams, consultants and contractors to deliver projects safely and successfully within agreed scope, budget and timeframes. Monitoring environmental and sustainability performance and contributing to institutional reporting, compliance obligations and strategic objectives. Developing practical sustainability policies, guidelines and resources that enable better environmental decision-making across UQ. Bringing sustainability to life for the UQ community through engaging communication, education and behaviour-change initiatives. This is an opportunity to see the impact of your work around you, from new biodiversity initiatives and smarter resource use to reduced waste and improved environmental performance across UQ. About You You’re a sustainability professional who combines technical knowledge with the ability to make things happen . You can spot an opportunity, investigate what is possible, bring the right people together and develop a credible pathway from concept to delivery. You’re equally comfortable working through data and compliance requirements, developing a business case, engaging senior stakeholders or coordinating the practical delivery of a project. You’ll bring curiosity, pragmatism and a genuine motivation to create measurable environmental outcomes. To be successful, you will demonstrate: A bachelor degree in Engineering, Environmental Management, Environmental Science, Business Management or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience developing and delivering sustainability, environmental management, water, waste, biodiversity and/or behaviour-change initiatives. Strong project management capability, including managing multiple priorities, stakeholders, budgets and deadlines. Highly developed analytical, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to translate complex sustainability issues into clear recommendations and influence diverse audiences. Advanced knowledge of sustainability principles, environmental management practices and relevant compliance obligations. A proactive approach to identifying challenges and opportunities, developing practical solutions and progressing initiatives with a high degree of independence. Experience developing business cases, funding submissions, grant applications and/or project proposals. Demonstrated ability to build productive relationships across organisational boundaries and coordinate multidisciplinary teams towards shared outcomes. A current open Queensland Driver Licence. You don’t need to have worked in a university before. We’re interested in the sustainability expertise, ideas and experience you can bring from complex environments across government, infrastructure, property, facilities, consulting, industry or other large organisations. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds from around the world, working together to create a better future. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. For a sustainability professional, this is a particularly exciting time to join UQ. Our campuses are living, evolving communities encompassing buildings, infrastructure, landscapes and natural environments, with thousands of people interacting with them every day. This creates complex sustainability challenges, but also an extraordinary opportunity to test ideas, build partnerships and deliver initiatives with visible impact. UQ’s Sustainability Framework and Plan sets an ambitious direction for coordinated action across the University, including a focus on regenerative campus systems, resilience and applied innovation . In this role, you’ll have the opportunity to help turn those ambitions into practical, measurable outcomes. UQ offers a range of benefits, including 17% superannuation, up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary carer leave, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working arrangements, health and wellbeing programs, heavily discounted Study for Staff, on-campus childcare, affordable parking and salary packaging options. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Jordan Forrest at jordan.forrest@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , quoting R-68824 in the subject line. When applying, please upload a resume and cover letter outlining how your skills and experience align with the requirements outlined in the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Applications close Sunday 27 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68824)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Network-Engineer_R-67782-1": {
      "title": "Senior Network Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nInformation Technology Services (ITS) Full-time, fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7)  Based at our St Lucia location    About This Opportunity    Join UQ’s Information Technology Services department as a Senior Network Engineer and play a pivotal role in designing, delivering and supporting the University's communications network that underpins teaching, learning, research and engagement across UQ. Working within a collaborative and high-performing team, you will contribute to enterprise-scale network infrastructure, lead technical initiatives, and help drive secure, reliable and innovative technology services across a complex and diverse environment. You will be empowered to shape solutions, influence outcomes, and work alongside talented professionals who are creating change together. Key responsibilities include: Design, implement and maintain enterprise network infrastructure to support the University's operational, teaching and research objectives. Lead the technical design and delivery of significant network and infrastructure projects, including the evaluation and adoption of emerging technologies. Provide expert technical advice and consultancy services to stakeholders, ensuring high-quality and customer-focused outcomes. Promote cyber security best practice and contribute to the ongoing reliability, resilience and performance of UQ's network environment. About You    You are an experienced network engineering professional who combines strong technical capability with a collaborative and solutions-focused approach. You thrive in complex environments, enjoy solving challenging problems, and are passionate about delivering secure, reliable and innovative technology services that create value for stakeholders. A degree in electrical, electronic or computer engineering, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience designing, deploying and supporting contemporary network infrastructure. Cisco CCNA certification or extensive hands-on experience with Cisco networking technologies. Strong knowledge of networking technologies such as network security, BGP, MPLS, IP Telephony, wireless networking or disaster recovery.   Proven ability to investigate, evaluate and implement new technologies and technical solutions. Excellent interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build effective stakeholder relationships. A strong customer service ethos and a methodical, organised approach to problem solving. The ability to work independently, contribute to team success, and lead technical activities or project outcomes when required. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Scott Dukeson, Senior Manager, ITS Infrastructure Build and Delivery at s.dukeson@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Wednesday 23 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67782)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Senior-Principal--Communications-and-Engagement_R-68579-1": {
      "title": "Senior Principal, Communications and Engagement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nHealth Translational Queensland Full-time, fixed-term position through to November 2026, with the possibility of extension subject to organisational requirements. Internal UQ applicants are encouraged to apply and may be considered for a secondment opportunity.    Base salary will be in the range $120,317 - $ 134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8)  Based at our  Herston Campus About This Opportunity    Are you ready to elevate your leadership profile and gain exposure to one of Queensland's most influential health and research networks? This is a unique opportunity to join Health Translation Queensland (HTQ) as Manager, Communications & Engagement, where you will lead strategic communications and engagement initiatives that showcase the impact of health research and drive collaboration across Queensland's health, research and academic sectors. Ideal for an established communications professional seeking to broaden their experience, this role offers significant strategic exposure, the opportunity to work with senior leaders and partner organisations, and the chance to lead high-profile projects with purpose and impact. Combining strategic leadership with hands-on delivery, it provides an exceptional platform to strengthen your stakeholder engagement expertise, expand your professional network and make a meaningful contribution to improving healthcare outcomes for Queensland communities. Key responsibilities will include:    Leading the development and delivery of strategic communications, marketing and engagement plans that strengthen HTQ's profile and advance organisational priorities. Driving impactful campaigns, content and stakeholder engagement initiatives that foster collaboration across Queensland's health, research and academic sectors. Overseeing HTQ's digital presence, including website, social media channels and communication platforms, ensuring a consistent, engaging and contemporary brand experience. Providing expert advice and leadership on communications, media, marketing and engagement best practice, while measuring performance and demonstrating impact. About You    Demonstrated success leading the development, implementation and evaluation of strategic communications, marketing and stakeholder engagement initiatives that enhance organisational profile and deliver measurable outcomes. Proven ability to build trusted relationships, influence stakeholders and work effectively across complex, multi-partner environments with professionalism, diplomacy and sound judgement. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to develop compelling content and tailor messaging for diverse audiences and stakeholder groups. Significant experience managing digital communication channels, including websites, social media, e-newsletters and content management platforms, while ensuring brand consistency and audience engagement. Demonstrated experience delivering integrated communication, marketing and engagement campaigns, including content creation, media engagement, events and brand management. Relevant tertiary qualifications in communications, marketing, public relations or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of extensive experience and expertise in contemporary communications and engagement practice. About Health Translation Queensland Health Translation Queensland (HTQ) is a partnership organisation bringing together 13 of Queensland’s world-class universities, research institutes and health services to help translate well-evidenced research into clinical practice. At HTQ, we connect the people and ideas needed to turn research into better health service delivery. By linking researchers with clinicians and consumers, we help tackle health service priorities and drive improvements in clinical practice. As a National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) accredited Research Translation Centre, we work to build the capability of our research translation workforce and to remove barriers to collaboration to accelerate research translation into clinical practice. With the support of our partners, we’re ultimately helping Queensland’s health system become more connected, capable, innovative and responsive through research translation. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Lauren Jeffery | Senior Manager, Program Operations and Partnerships at lauren.jeffery@healthtranslationqld.org.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Wednesday 16 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68579).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the end of September 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Scientist--Analytics-_R-68720-1": {
      "title": "Research Scientist (Analytics)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nNational Biologics Facility , based in the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to 31 December 2027. Base salary will be in the range $85,452 to $94,984 + 17% super (HEW Level 5). Based at our St Lucia Campus . About This Opportunity The National Biologics Facility is seeking a passionate and collaborative Research Scientist to provide specialist technical expertise within its Analytics team. Working in an innovative research environment supporting discovery, bioprocessing (upstream and downstream) and quality assurance, you will execute analytical assays that characterise recombinant protein biologics and support their development as therapeutic agents. This is an opportunity to apply your passion and scientific expertise to translational research and help advance the next generation of innovative therapeutics and protein research. Key responsibilities will include: Scientific Execute analytical assays for characterisation of quality attributes of recombinant protein biologics Assist with qualification of analytical assays for batch release and stability testing Undertake protein formulation studies Peer review of Test Methods and Batch Reports for internal quality assurance Communicate results in detailed batch reports and presentations to internal and external stakeholders Support the development of more junior research engineers, scientists, technicians, and research higher degree students Assist with wider NBF activities as required, including cross-functional support across discovery, upstream, and downstream teams Any other duties as reasonably directed by your supervisor Quality Assist with maintaining the Facility’s Quality Systems, including authoring and following Standard Operating Procedures with a high attention to detail Assist in the maintenance and daily operation of the laboratory, clean room facilities and equipment About You Qualifications and training equivalent to a Bachelor of Science (Hons) in a relevant area (Bioengineering, Bioprocessing, Chemical Engineering, Pharmacy, Molecular Biology, Biochemistry) Experience in analytical characterisation of recombinant proteins; experience in assay development and qualification is desirable. Demonstrated competency in executing experimental procedures, record keeping and interpretation of results Well-developed problem-solving skills, with an ability to forward plan, manage projects, prioritise tasks to meet deadlines and work with a high level of adaptability, both independently and as part of a team Experience in generating, following and maintaining Standard Operating Procedures, record keeping and preparation of reports Responsible approach to equipment use and maintenance Computer literacy including experience with standard office software and electronic databases Strong interpersonal and communication skills with proven ability to produce clear, succinct documentation Knowledge of the biologics manufacturing industry in GMP environments is desirable, as is experience in writing Standard Operating Procedures and experience working under a Quality Management System About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Mr Alex Pinder at a.pinder@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, mandatory immunisations checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close 27 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68720)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Long-Pocket/Senior-Software-Developer_R-68450-1": {
      "title": "Senior Software Developer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAUSCERT Full-time, permanent position Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7)  Based at our Long Pocket location    About This Opportunity    Join AUSCERT and help create change together. We are seeking a Senior Software Developer to provide high-level software development, technical leadership, and ongoing support for critical cybersecurity systems and services. Working within a collaborative, inclusive, and purpose-driven team, you will contribute to the design, development, deployment, and maintenance of systems that support one of the world’s longest-running Computer Emergency Response Teams. This is an exciting opportunity to work alongside experienced cybersecurity and technology professionals, leveraging modern cloud and software engineering practices to deliver solutions that help organisations prevent, detect, and respond to cyber threats. Key responsibilities will include: Designing, implementing, deploying, and maintaining information technology systems and supporting infrastructure. Leading the design and development of significant software projects and providing technical expertise across software architecture, deployment, and maintenance. Investigating, evaluating, and recommending emerging technologies, systems, standards, and processes to support continuous improvement. Building strong partnerships with clients and stakeholders while collaborating with software developers and multidisciplinary teams to deliver high-quality outcomes. This is a continuing position that will play a critical role in supporting AUSCERT's cybersecurity services and technology platforms. About You    You are a collaborative and solutions-focused software development professional who enjoys working in a dynamic environment, building high-quality technology solutions, and mentoring others. You bring strong technical capability, a customer-focused mindset, and a genuine interest in continuous learning and innovation. A degree in Information Technology or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of experience and training. Experience designing, developing, and supporting complex software applications and systems. Strong Python development skills, including frameworks such as Django or NiceGUI. Experience with relational and NoSQL databases, including PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, or DynamoDB. Experience working with modern JavaScript frameworks such as React, Angular, or Vue. Hands-on experience developing and deploying solutions in AWS and/or Azure environments. Knowledge of software engineering best practices, Agile delivery methods, and Git version control. Well-developed communication skills with the ability to collaborate, mentor others, and manage competing priorities. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Josh Hopkins, Developer Team Lead at josh.hopkins@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 20 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68450)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Fellow---First-Nations-Health--Identified-s25-_R-68544-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Fellow - First Nations Health (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFirst Nations Cancer and Wellbeing Research Program within the School of Public Health Two, full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level A/B) This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement due to the role's focus on conducting and leading research with community to improve health and wellbeing outcomes. Based at our Herston Campus . Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity  Join the First Nations Cancer and Wellbeing Research (FNCWR) Program and contribute to innovative, community-driven research that is improving health and wellbeing outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Working within a collaborative and purpose-led research environment, you will partner with communities, health services and research stakeholders to deliver impactful research that addresses inequities across the cancer continuum. We are seeking two Early Carers Academics to work on two research projects: Postdoctoral Research Fellow (level A) Contribute to a Cancer Australia Funded project (GNT: 2024/PCR/2045026), the Empowering Indigenous Communities: Strengthening Agency, Equity and Connections in Cancer Care and Prevention (CASE) project, a newly established initiative focused on improving health literacy and access to cancer prevention, early detection, care and treatment for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in Northern Queensland. This role offers the opportunity to help shape a new project, build strong stakeholder relationships and develop your research profile within a supportive team environment. Research Fellow (level B) Take a leadership role in the NHMRC funded (GNT 2027475) project, the Miyil-Janay Yundu project, which aims to improve equity in cancer screening participation for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. You will provide leadership across project delivery, stakeholder engagement, research translation and team management, while contributing strategically to the broader goals of the FNCWR Program. In both positions, you will have the opportunity to contribute to meaningful projects, build valuable partnerships and help shape the future of First Nations health research. Key responsibilities will include:  Research. Develop and contribute to high-impact research programs, manage and analyse data, apply best-practice methodologies, and communicate findings to expert, community and general audiences through quality publications and presentations. Research funding, engagement and translation. Contribute to competitive funding applications, build collaborative research projects, engage meaningfully with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and stakeholders, and support the translation of findings into policy, practice and improved health outcomes. Supervision and Researcher Development. Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, provide constructive feedback and coaching, and support research staff where appropriate. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You  The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The position is therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will join a Group of Eight university recognised for excellence in teaching, research and engagement. UQ offers a supportive and rewarding academic environment, with benefits including recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible and hybrd working arrangements, access to exclusive internal‑only vacancies, cultural leave, and genuine career progression through the academic promotions process. You will also have opportunities to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, fostering a strong sense of community and belonging. UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by the University’s Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. Your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced, and you will be supported to contribute to an organisation that champions Indigenous voices. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Gail Garvey . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 4 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST ( R-68544 ) . Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Wednesday 21 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Toowong/Awards-Officer_R-68626-1": {
      "title": "Awards Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nResearch Office This is a full-time fixed-term position through to April 2027 with salary in the range $97,268 - $104,348 (HEW 6), plus a generous super allowance of up to 17%. Support the end to end post-award lifecycle for UQ's research grants through the accurate administration of grant agreements, project establishment, project variations, reporting requirements and compliance processes that enable research success. Join a high performing team where your initiative and customer focused approach will ensure seamless compliance and proactive partnership management. Based at our conveniently located Toowong High Street office ( occasional travel to other UQ campuses will be required ). About This Opportunity  The Awards Officer plays a critical role in supporting UQ's post-award research funding function through the accurate administration, establishment and ongoing management of research grants. Working in a fast-paced, high-volume environment, you will spend a significant portion of your day coordinating grant documentation, processing project establishment activities, managing project variations, monitoring reporting obligations and ensuring compliance with University and funding provider requirements. You will work closely with researchers, funding bodies and professional staff across UQ, balancing detailed administrative work with stakeholder engagement to enable successful research outcomes. Key responsibilities will include:  Administer a high volume of post-award research funding activities, including project establishment, grant agreement processing, project variations and reporting requirements. Review, prepare and coordinate research funding documentation, ensuring compliance with University policies, procedures and funding provider requirements. Maintain accurate records, monitor project milestones and contractual obligations, and manage workflow tasks to support timely research activation and delivery. Liaise with researchers, funding providers and internal stakeholders to resolve administrative and funding management issues and provide professional advice and support. About You   You are a highly organised administrator who enjoys working in a detailed, process driven environment. You can manage competing priorities, maintain accuracy across large volumes of work, and build productive relationships with a broad range of stakeholders. Relevant qualifications and/or equivalent professional experience in research administration, grants administration, project administration, contract administration, compliance, governance or a related field. Previous experience in research grant administration or post-award research management will be highly regarded. Demonstrated experience managing high volumes of documentation, workflows and administrative processes while maintaining a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Experience reviewing, preparing and administering complex documents, with the ability to interpret and apply policies, procedures and contractual requirements in a complex environment. Strong organisational, analytical and problem solving skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands, meet deadlines and identify practical solutions to funding management issues. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with a proven ability to provide professional, customer-focused support and build effective working relationships across a diverse organisation. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary, inclusive and purposeful community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. You will be part of a connected network that is creating change together through global partnerships, interdisciplinary research and programs that address complex challenges. Enjoy 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and salary packaging. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary, inclusive and purposeful community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Cristina at c.sanchez@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au,  stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support accessibility requirements throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 20 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68626). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 28 September 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Manager--Research-Programs--Outcomes-and-Impact-_R-68748-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager (Research Programs, Outcomes and Impact)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUQ Research Office Full-time, permanent position. Base salary will be in the range $140,766 - $148,862 + 17% super (HEW Level 9). Based at our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus . About This Opportunity Help shape the future of research programs, outcomes and impact at one of the world's leading universities. The Research Office enhances UQ's research performance and profile by working with faculties, institutes, research teams and researchers to develop ambitious research programs, strengthen inter- and transdisciplinary research and expand participation in national and international research initiatives. As Senior Manager (Research Programs, Outcomes and Impact), you will play a pivotal role in advancing UQ's research performance, partnerships and global engagement through the development of large scale inter- and transdisciplinary, and international collaborative research programs. Working across institutional boundaries, you will help connect researchers, partners and opportunities to address complex societal challenges, strengthen research impact and position UQ for success in major national and international collaborative initiatives. Working within a collaborative Research Office team and closely with colleagues across faculties, institutes and central divisions, you will help you will help expand UQ's participation in international research and innovation ecosystems, including Horizon Europe and other strategically important global initiatives. You will contribute to the development of research programs, networks and collaborative platforms that enable UQ researchers to compete successfully in increasingly interconnected research environments. This role offers the opportunity to influence institution wide research initiatives and work with leading researchers and partners, philanthropic organisations and international collaborators across Australia and globally. Key responsibilities will include: Develop and deliver strategic initiatives, frameworks and collaborative mechanisms that strengthen UQ's inter- and transdisciplinary research programs and enhance the quality, scale, diversity and impact of the University's research portfolio. Develop and deliver initiatives that diversify and strengthen UQ's participation in national and international research and innovation ecosystems, including Horizon Europe, international foundations, mission-oriented research programs and other strategic collaborative initiatives. Facilitate the development of strategic partnerships and collaborative networks across academia, industry, government, philanthropy and international organisations that address significant societal and global challenges. Identify and enable opportunities for large scale collaborative research programs, supporting researchers to build consortia, develop the required partnerships and pursue ambitious research agendas. Provide expert leadership, capability building, strategic advice and project delivery support that strengthen research outcomes, impact, knowledge exchange and institutional performance. About You You are a strategic, collaborative and adaptable professional who thrives in dynamic environments and enjoys bringing people together around ambitious ideas. You have a demonstrated ability to influence outcomes, build partnerships and translate complex opportunities into impactful research initiatives. Essential A postgraduate qualification in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of extensive experience, education and training, together with demonstrated success leading complex strategic initiatives, collaborative programs or major projects in a research, higher education, government or related environment. Proven experience developing and delivering major funding proposals, business cases, investment opportunities or strategic initiatives that secure organisational outcomes, resources, investment or external engagement. Strong knowledge of the Australian research and innovation sector, together with experience navigating international research funding landscapes and collaborative funding mechanisms. Experience with Horizon Europe or comparable international research funding programs will be viewed favourably. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, relationship management and influencing skills, with the ability to build productive partnerships across academia, industry, government, philanthropy and international organisations. Exceptional written communication, strategic thinking and problem-solving capabilities, with the agility and adaptability to thrive in evolving research, policy and innovation environments. Desirable Experience supporting, developing or participating in Horizon Europe or other major multinational research funding programs involving complex international consortia and cross-sector collaboration. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you'll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a collaborative, inclusive and purposeful community where excellence sits at the heart of everything we do and where your contribution can create meaningful impact locally and globally. UQ continues to strengthen its global research partnerships and international engagement. Recent international collaboration initiatives have demonstrated the University's commitment to working with leading organisations around the world to address significant societal challenges, creating pathways for research excellence and real world impact. Join a community where interdisciplinary collaboration is encouraged, innovation is celebrated and talented people are empowered to create change together. Enjoy a range of benefits and rewards including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Jodi Clyde-Smith , Executive Director, Research Office at j.clyde-smith@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au,  stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the 'About You' section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We're also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 27 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68748). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 6 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---Institute-of-Modern-Languages_R-69033": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - Institute of Modern Languages",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity      The University of Queensland’s Institute of Modern Languages is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics to be engaged on a casual basis.   Casual Academics make a rich and important contribution to the Institutes ’ language teaching and community engagement endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):    Teaching    Personalised Tutoring    Development of teaching materials   Exam Marking   Preparation of lesson plans   Report writing and other associated duties.   What We Can Offer      These are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.     For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) .     Eligibility Qualifications   A Bachelor level degree or other qualification in teaching, education, TESOL, languages or linguistics . Or a certificate IV in Training and Assessment or equivalent.   Knowledge and Skills   Native speaker or native-like proficiency in the target language.   Ability to develop programs for specific language learning p urposes .   Ability to evaluate & assess student progress.   Knowledge of current practices in language teaching methodology .   Experience   General or Language teaching experience.   Personal qualities   Good communication and interpersonal skills.   Ability to adopt a flexible approach to teaching style in response to student needs   You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. UQ’s Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to casualrecruit@uq.edu.au .     Want to Apply?     All applicants will be asked to nominate their course and/or general subject area preference/s in the application form. In addition, all applicants must upload the following documents for an application to be considered:   A copy of your current CV/resume   A brief statement addressing:   experience in tutoring/facilitating /marking exams ; experience using online tools (Padlet, Zoom, Teams); knowledge/expertise in the target language.  Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box during the application form. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board.   Questions?    To discuss the role please contact the [ Pru Rolff ] via email [ p.rolff@uq.edu.au ]     For technical queries about your application, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number   in the subject line."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Crop-Genomics-Research-Technician_R-68290-1": {
      "title": "Crop Genomics Research Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation . Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,452 to $94,984 + 17% super (HEW Level 5). Based at our St Lucia Campus . About This Opportunity Join a collaborative team of molecular biologists, geneticists, bioinformaticians and crop researchers supporting two complementary sorghum and sugarcane research projects. As the Crop Genomics Research Technician, you will coordinate laboratory samples and data, undertake molecular assays with technical independence, and help deliver reliable research outcomes that advance the identification and validation of genes and alleles affecting biomass composition, tillering and other important physiological traits. Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Plan and undertake molecular laboratory workflows, including plant tissue sampling, DNA extraction, DNA quantification, quality assessment, PCR, genotyping and validation assays. Coordinate the receipt, labelling, storage, tracking and movement of samples across laboratory, glasshouse, field and sequencing workflows, maintaining accurate chain-of-custody and project records. Design or assist with primers and probes, optimise targeted molecular assays, troubleshoot routine technical issues, and develop or adapt laboratory protocols and standard operating procedures. Perform quality control and preliminary analysis of laboratory data, maintain project inventories, support safe laboratory operations, and contribute to team planning, milestone reporting and practical guidance for students and colleagues. About You A degree in molecular biology, plant science, biotechnology or a related discipline. Demonstrated experience in plant molecular biology laboratory methods, including DNA extraction, DNA quality assessment, targeted PCR and SNP assays, and primer design. Demonstrated ability to organise complex sample workflows and maintain accurate laboratory, sample and research-data records, with strong attention to quality, traceability and detail. Technical capability in implementing and optimising assays, with the judgement to troubleshoot routine problems and seek advice when issues are novel or have broader project implications. Experience supporting field or glasshouse sampling and linking phenotypic records with molecular samples. Experience using relevant laboratory equipment and software, together with sound computer literacy, including Microsoft Office and laboratory, sample or research-data management systems. Strong interpersonal and written communication skills, with the ability to contribute effectively within multidisciplinary teams and provide clear practical guidance to students and colleagues. Demonstrated ability to plan and prioritise concurrent activities, meet research deadlines, and work safely and reliably with limited day-to-day supervision. Experience with sorghum, sugarcane, other grasses or crop-research materials, digital PCR, copy-number assays, targeted sequencing, high-throughput sample processing or primer and probe design would be advantageous. A current, valid Class C driver’s licence is essential, with the ability to travel between University, field, glasshouse and other research sites as required. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a purposeful, inclusive and collaborative community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Karen Aitken karen.aitken@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close 20 September at 11.00pm AEST (R-68290). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for week commencing 28 September."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Project-Manager--Health-Professional-_R-67745-1": {
      "title": "Casual Project Manager (Health Professional)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nSchool of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $79.51 - $89.14 (HEW Level 8) + 12% Superannuation Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity  We are eagerly seeking an allied health professional (i.e. Occupational Therapist), with project management experience, to join our School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences on a casual basis. As the Project Manager , you will have the opportunity to support the strategic and operational management of the SPEF-R2 Online platform, a nationally used student assessment and placement management system. In this position, you will lead stakeholder engagement and act as the primary liaison with participating universities for both project communications and routine troubleshooting for placement supervisors as system users. You will coordinate compliance activities and project manage improvement and upgrade initiatives to achieve alignment with accreditation requirements, and deliver outcomes across all aspects of the project life cycle including: Project initiation and planning Project administration and management Stakeholder engagement and communication Website system support Project governance and reporting Financial management This is a casual role in which the hours of work will vary from week to week dependent upon project demands. Applicants will need to be available to work flexible hours in response to these demands. While we anticipate an average of approximately 8 hours of work per week, hours may increase over the coming months as project activity grows and business needs evolve. To support our successful applicant, a comprehensive handover will be provided. Flexible work arrangements may also be available, including the option to work from home following a suitable induction period, and flexible start and finish times, subject to operational requirements and agreed core working hours. About You  Our ideal applicant will be an allied health professional with: Demonstrated experience successfully managing projects with a range of stakeholders, ideally within a large and/or complex and/or health/education organisational context. Experience coordinating project working groups, including planning, organising, and monitoring outcomes. Demonstrated high level organisational ability, attention to detail and ability to work to tight deadlines and manage competing priorities.  Demonstrated knowledge of relevant project management tools or equivalent practical methodology for monitoring project progress. High-level interpersonal skills, including the effective liaison and negotiation skills needed to develop and maintain strong working relationships with the governance team, the software vendor, and the institutions using the tool.   Interested? We encourage you to reach out to discuss this unique and rewarding role with either Professor Jodie Copley (Head of Occupational Therapy) or Wendy Hood (Project Manager). Both can be contacted on info@spefr.online .   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-67745) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter (max 3 pages). In your cover letter, please summarise how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above and provide an indication of your weekly availability. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia and your professional body registration or membership (i.e. AHPRA). This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.  You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.  We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 24 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67745). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Manager--Accessibility-and-Welfare_R-67981-2": {
      "title": "Manager, Accessibility and Welfare",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAccess and Inclusion team (S tudent S upport and W ellbeing S ervices )   Drive meaningful change through accessibility, inclusion and student wellbeing leadership.   Full-time , permanent with b ase salary in the range of $ 120,317 - $ 134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8 ) .     Based at our v ibrant St Lucia Campus.       About This Opportunity      Join The University of Queensland ’s Student Support and Wellbeing Services team in a pivotal leadership role , where you'll help shape a more inclusive, accessible and supportive student experience. Reporting to the Senior Manager, Access and Inclusion, you'll lead the multidisciplinary teams delivering accessibility, welfare, diversity and inclusion services that empower students to fully participate and succeed in university life.    This is an opportunity to help shape accessibility and welfa re practice at UQ and across the sector . Combining strategic leadership with service excellence, you'll influence how UQ supports students to overcome barriers to access and participation , while leading initiatives that strengthen the University's commitment to equity, inclusion and wellbeing.   Key responsibilities will include:       Leading and developing multidisciplinary teams delivering accessibility, welfare, disability, diversity and inclusion services to students.     Driving strategic and operational initiatives that enhance student access, participation and success.     Partnering with stakeholders across UQ to foster a coordinated, student-centred approach to service delivery.     Leading service improvement, change management and process redesign initiatives to strengthen support outcomes.   Providing specialist advice on accessibility, welfare and inclusion matters, informed by legislative and policy requirements.     Building a culture of wellbeing, psychosocial safety, accountability and continuous professional growth.     About You       You are an experienced and values-driven leader who is passionate about creating equitable opportunities and improving outcomes for diverse communities. You bring a collaborative leadership style, strong stakeholder engagement skills and the ability to navigate dynamic environments while maintaining a clear focus on people, service quality and impact.    You will demonstrate :   Experience leading multidisciplinary teams delivering client-centred health, welfare or support services.   E xperience implementing disability-related legislation and standards into organisational policy, process and service delivery, gained in tertiary, education, government, health, community services or another relevant sector .   Strong relationship-building and influencing skills, with the ability to work effectively across large and dynamic organisations.    Experience leading projects, service improvements and organisational change initiatives.    Strategic thinking, sound judgement and the ability to balance competing priorities in a fast-paced environment.    Excellent communication skills and the ability to inspire, motivate and develop high-performing teams.    A commitment to accessibility, inclusion, student wellbeing and fostering positive outcomes for diverse communities.        This role includes the opportunity and expectation to travel to UQ campuses at Dutton Park, Gatton and Herston while being predominantly based at St Lucia.     About UQ     As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.         Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on ‑ campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.        Interested ?     For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dominique Henry on dominique.henry@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.           When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and a personalised c over letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.      Other Information       Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.         You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.         We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.        Applications close Monday 21 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R- 67981 ). Applications received via email will not be accepted. Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 28 th September ."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Mater-Misericordiae-Hospitals--QCH-South-Brisbane/Advanced-Clinical-Technician---Nursing---Midwifery_R-68714-1": {
      "title": "Advanced Clinical Technician - Nursing & Midwifery",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Nursing, Midwifery and Social Work Full-time, continuing position commencing ASAP! Base salary will range from $85,452 to $94,984 (HEW Level 5) + 17% Super Primarily based at Mater Misericordiae Hospital in South Brisbane however work will be required across multiple locations About This Opportunity Join a purposeful and collaborative clinical education environment where your expertise will help nursing and midwifery students build confidence, strengthen practical skills and prepare for clinical assessment. As an Advanced Clinical Technician , you will support students, clinical lecturers and course coordinators across multiple Brisbane teaching hospital locations, helping to maintain safe, well-equipped and inclusive simulated learning spaces where people can work together to create meaningful change. Key responsibilities will include: Prepare and support the delivery of Clinical Skills sessions and assist students to master specific clinical skills during self-directed learning. Liaise with clinical units, clinical lecturers, course coordinators and company representatives to coordinate learning activities, equipment demonstrations and quotations. Provide targeted remedial support to nursing and midwifery students identified as requiring additional assistance Maintain clinical equipment and consumables, while guiding others in appropriate equipment-cleaning practices. Contribute to stocktakes, workplace inspections, facilities requests and the review of equipment procedures. About You Completion of an undergraduate degree, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education and training. Experience in clinical health care environment highly desirable. Demonstrated experience maintaining and using medical equipment. Knowledge of nursing and midwifery equipment and medical supplies, or the ability to rapidly develop this knowledge. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks and deliver a high level of customer service. Excellent interpersonal, written and oral communication skills, with demonstrated experience liaising effectively with people at all levels of an organisation. The ability to work collaboratively, build effective relationships and adapt constructively to changing priorities. Familiarity with Microsoft Office applications and experience with online ordering systems, or the capacity to quickly develop these skills. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible start/finish times, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, and salary packaging options. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sharon Barlow at s.barlow@uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-68714 . When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, a valid driver’s licence and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-68714)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Neuroscience_R-69089-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Neuroscience",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nQueensland Brain Institute Full-time (100% FTE), fixed-term position for up to 12 months commencing ASAP Base salary will range from $85,373 to $113,660 (Academic Level A) + 17% Super Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity We are seeking a motivated and driven Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join our world-leading Queensland Brain Institute. In this research-focused role , you will contribute to the development of novel monoclonal antibody therapies for Alzheimer’s disease. Working within a collaborative and high-performing neuroscience team, you will design and conduct in vitro and in vivo studies to validate antibodies targeting tau and help translate innovative discoveries into therapeutic outcomes. Join a vibrant research community of leading scientists, researchers and students dedicated to advancing neuroscience and creating meaningful impact through world-class discovery and translation. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Design and conduct research to improve and functionally validate antibodies targeting tau in neurodegeneration in vitro and in mouse models, undertake data analysis, prepare manuscripts, publish high-quality research and present findings at laboratory, institutional, national and international meetings, and prepare reports for the funders. Funding and Research Development: Apply for independent and collaborative research funding, maintain familiarity with relevant scientific literature and contribute actively to the disciplined academic environment of the laboratory and institute. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of junior researchers and students, support responsible research practices and help enable the smooth running of the laboratory. Citizenship and Service: Contribute to a safe, inclusive and collegial laboratory environment, participate in priority activities for the laboratory and institute, and uphold University values, policies and responsible research practices. About You Completion of a PhD in Biochemistry, Protein Engineering, Neuroscience or any relevant discipline. Demonstrated experience in protein chemistry, recombinant protein expression and cell culture. Demonstrated proficiency in oral and written communication. Publication(s) in reputable refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Experience contributing to meaningful internal service activities, collaborative research environments or external engagement. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Jürgen Götz, NHMRC Leadership Fellow, at j.goetz@uq.edu.au or Dr Esteban Cruz, antibody development leader e.cruz@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69089. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter including responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of the right to work in Australia and your qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close 13th October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69089)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Aquatic-Technical--Coordinator_R-68380": {
      "title": "Aquatic Technical, Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nBiological Resources Full-time, permanent position Base salary will be in the range $97,269 - $104,349 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 6)  Based at our St. Lucia Campus About This Opportunity    The University of Queensland is seeking an experienced Aquatic Technical Coordinator to play a pivotal role in supervising staff and coordinating the day-to-day delivery of high-quality Aquatic animal husbandry and research support services within a complex, regulated research environment. This position will require a working knowledge of aquaculture systems, ability to participate in routine operations (including rostered weekend and afterhours On-Call support), provision of technical leadership across aquatic laboratory animal operations, support for research project planning, and the highest standards of animal welfare, biosafety, and regulatory compliance. Working closely with researchers, students, and technical staff, you will combine hands-on aquaculture expertise with supervision and training responsibilities, contributing directly to the success of cutting-edge research and the safe, efficient operation of specialised research facilities. Key responsibilities will include:  Provide on-the-ground technical support for routine tasks and coordinate dynamic schedules across complex facilities (facilitating husbandry, animal health, service delivery and team development) Strong organisational skills with the ability to prioritise competing demands, ability to work efficiently and independently in line with Standard Operation Procedures and achieve deadlines. Support effective facility operation through collaboration with management on service delivery, staff supervision, training and operational procedure development Deliver expert Aquatic animal husbandry and technical research support, including the support of breeding programs, advanced procedures, animal welfare and regulatory compliance. Participate in regular rostered weekend and on-call shifts (average 2 per month). About You   A relevant degree (e.g. animal science) or substantial specialist experience within the animal technology or research sector, with the ability to operate effectively in complex research environments. Ability to support teams across highly regulated facilities, with well-developed problem-solving skills and adaptability within dynamic environments. Experience supervising, training, and mentoring staff or students, with a collaborative approach to building capability and maintaining high operational standards. Demonstrated commitment to best-practice animal welfare, husbandry, biosafety, and biosecurity, supported by sound knowledge of regulatory and ethical frameworks. Proven experience drafting and following Standard Operating Procedures, including accurate record keeping and compliance-driven data management. Hands-on technical capability, including experience with euthanasia and exposure to advanced animal procedures, considered advantageous. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options where operationally appropriate, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact Gillian Lawrence, Aquatics Facility Manager, at g.lawrence1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.   Applications close Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68380)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Student-and-Academic-Administration-Officer---Civil-Engineering_R-68908-1": {
      "title": "Student and Academic Administration Officer - Civil Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nSchool of Civil Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Part-time (50%), fixed-term position through to 7 May 2027 Base full-time salary will be in the range $85,451.97 - $94,983.84 + 17% super (HEW Level 5) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  We are seeking a motivated part-time Student and Academic Administration Officer to provide high-quality customer service through the delivery of friendly, helpful, accurate, and informative advice and services to students, professional and academic staff. You will be responsible for completing a diverse range of administrative processing tasks and associated day-to-day problem resolution and maintenance of administrative systems. The current staff member will be going on parental leave and therefore the role is only offered on a fixed term basis. Key responsibilities will include:   Provide accurate administrative support for student and academic processes, including applications, enrolment, and course coordination. Offer advice on student matters, maintain university systems and information, and assist with process improvements and updates. You will also provide secretarial support, assist with process improvements, represent the University at events, prepare reports, and undertake other duties as required. About You   An undergraduate degree in a relevant area or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training. A sound understanding of administrative processes relevant to student and academic administration, or the ability to rapidly gain such knowledge. Demonstrated ability to establish priorities and meet deadlines. High level of attention to detail and accuracy in written work. Ability to exercise initiative and judgment and to work independently and autonomously. High level of verbal and interpersonal communication skills. The ability to work collaboratively and effectively within the team and across other organisational units. A strong orientation to the provision of a high level of customer service. An empathy with and ability to communicate with students, including those from diverse cultural backgrounds. Experience in a tertiary education institution or other large organisation in an area related to student administration would be highly regarded. Knowledge of University student computer systems and packages e.g. Microsoft Office, SI-net, Business Objects, InPlace and the use of CRM systems would be desirable. We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Jeannette Watson via jeannette@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday, 29 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68908)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/University-Centre---Toowoomba/Reporting-and-Data-Analyst_R-68832-1": {
      "title": "Reporting and Data Analyst",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nSouthern Queensland Rural Health Part-time (60% FTE), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will range from $97,268 to $104,348 (HEW Level 6) + 17% Super Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander applicants are strongly encouraged to apply Based at SQRH Toowoomba About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team working to improve health outcomes across rural, regional and remote Queensland by helping to strengthen the rural health workforce. As the Reporting and Data Analyst , you will collect, analyse, validate and report data across key Southern Queensland Rural Health programs and services. Working closely with clinical, operational and executive stakeholders, you will turn complex information into timely, meaningful insights that support operational management, strategic planning, funding obligations and organisational performance. You will also help create change through improved data quality, intelligent dashboards, automated reporting and accessible business intelligence solutions. Key responsibilities will include: Coordinate accurate and timely reporting for the Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training program and support reporting processes for the Nursing and Allied Health Graduate Outcomes Tracking program. Produce clinic activity, utilisation and performance reports across clinical, educational, student support and operational services. Develop and maintain dashboards and visualisations that enable stakeholders to monitor organisational performance, service delivery and program outcomes. Analyse trends, identify variations and improve reporting efficiency through automation, standardisation and self-service reporting solutions. About You Completion of a degree in information management, business analytics, data analytics, statistics, information technology or a related discipline, with subsequent relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education or training. Demonstrated experience in data extraction, analysis, interpretation and reporting within a complex organisational environment. Experience developing and maintaining reports, dashboards and data visualisations using Microsoft Power BI, Excel, SQL or similar reporting tools. Demonstrated ability to develop performance measures, dashboards and reports that support operational and strategic decision-making. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to identify trends, patterns, variations and opportunities for improvement. Demonstrated experience maintaining data quality and undertaking validation and quality assurance activities. Highly developed written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to present information clearly to technical and non-technical audiences. Proven ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and build collaborative working relationships across a diverse organisation. About UQ UQ offers an inclusive, collaborative and ambitious workplace where professional staff play a vital role in enabling teaching excellence, world class research and meaningful community impact. Join a community where your contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as: 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave 17% superannuation contributions  17.5% annual leave loading  Health and wellness discounts – discounted health insurance and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family  Access to professional development opportunities such as UQ Study for Staff  Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact David Fawcett (SQRH Senior Manager, Operations) at david.fawcett@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-68832 ) . When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of the right to work in Australia and may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 29 September at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-68832). Applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Aquatics-Technical-Assistant_R-68717-1": {
      "title": "Aquatics Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUQ Biological Resources Full-time (100%), permanent position. Base salary will be in the range $79,096 - $83,863 + 17% super (HEW Level 4). Based at our St Lucia location. About This Opportunity Join a collaborative, inclusive and purpose-driven team supporting world-class research through the delivery of high-quality animal care and technical services. As an Animal Technical Assistant, you will play a vital role in our team, maintaining consistent health, welfare and husbandry of laboratory research animals. Working within a professional and supportive environment, you will be contributing directly to the success of cutting-edge research by maintaining compliance, welfare and the safe, efficient operation of specialised research facilities. Key responsibilities will include: Providing routine animal care, husbandry and welfare support for laboratory aquatic species, including water quality maintenance. Performing manual housekeeping tasks to support facility hygiene and equipment operation Performing routine technical procedures, including approved euthanasia methods, in accordance with regulatory requirements and animal welfare standards. Maintaining accurate facility records and providing timely communication Participate in regular rostered weekend and on-call shifts (average 2 per month). About You A relevant qualification (e.g. Aquaculture, Animal Technology) with relevant experience. Experience with animal handling, recirculating aquaculture systems, equipment maintenance, animal health monitoring, and euthanasia practices. and/or laboratory animal environments, would be highly regarded. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build productive relationships with researchers, students, suppliers and colleagues. Strong organisational skills and the ability to prioritise competing demands, work efficiently and independently in line with Standard Operation Procedures and achieve deadlines. Experience in a scientific research environment and proficiency with Microsoft Word and Excel would be advantageous. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options where operationally appropriate, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Gillian Lawrence, Aquatics Facility Manager, at g.lawrence1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68717)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Storeperson_R-67583-1": {
      "title": "Storeperson",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $70,318.51 - $77,400.08 + 17% super (HEW Level 3). Based at our St Lucia location . About This Opportunity    As a Storeperson, you will play a practical and purposeful role in supporting the smooth movement of goods, materials and services across a fully site-based store environment. Working as part of a small, collaborative team, you will help keep daily operations running safely, accurately and efficiently, contributing to the responsive service culture that supports UQ’s broader community to create change together. Key responsibilities will include: Receiving, storing, handling and delivering chemicals, laboratory consumables and other goods in line with operational requirements and safe work practices. Processing client orders, goods receipts and store records accurately using relevant computer systems. Prioritising daily tasks to ensure goods and materials are prepared, moved and delivered in an efficient and timely manner. Providing professional, helpful and reliable service to clients, colleagues and stakeholders across the store environment.   About You    Completion of a trade certificate, Certificate III, or equivalent combination of qualifications and relevant work experience. Relevant stores, warehouse or goods handling experience. An unrestricted driver's licence and Queensland LF Forklift licence. Basic computer skills, including the ability to use Excel, or the capacity to quickly learn Excel, to accurately process orders and goods receipts. Good communication and customer service skills. The ability to work safely, prioritise tasks and meet deadlines. A collaborative approach and commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that candidates bring diverse experiences and career pathways. If you have any of the following, we encourage you to highlight them in your application: Current First Aid certification. Experience operating an EWP (under 11m). Experience handling dangerous goods and chemicals. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Audrey Ndukwe a.ndukwe@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, an unrestricted driver's licence and Queensland LF Forklift licence A and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close 5 October 2026  at 11.00pm AEST (R-67583).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for week commencing 12 October."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Academic-Lead--Clinical-Skills_R-68749-1": {
      "title": "Academic Lead, Clinical Skills",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nMedical School Part-time (0.5FTE), 2-year Fixed-Term position   Base salary will be in the range $145,961- $168,043 + 17% super (Academic Level C)  Based at our  Herston Campus About This Opportunity    Shape the foundations of tomorrow’s medical practice. As Academic Lead, Clinical Skills, you will play a pivotal role in designing and delivering an innovative, future-focused clinical skills curriculum with an initial focus on Years 1 and 2 of the UQ Doctor of Medicine program. Working within a collaborative environment, you will partner with academic leaders, clinicians, professional staff, and teaching teams across Learning Communities and clinical schools to ensure a seamlessly integrated and high-quality student experience. This is a unique opportunity to influence how aspiring doctors develop the core capabilities that underpin safe, compassionate, and effective clinical care, while contributing to a culture of excellence, inclusivity, and continuous improvement. Key responsibilities will include :   Teaching:  Lead and deliver the Clinical Skills curriculum in the MD Program with particular focus on Years 1 and 2, including curriculum design, teaching, assessment, educator development, and ensuring alignment with whole of program clinical skills outcomes and university standards. Research:  Apply and contribute to evidence-based medical education through scholarship of teaching and learning, collaborative projects, and the development of innovative curriculum and teaching practices. Supervision and Researcher Development:  Lead, mentor, and support academic staff and educators, including performance development, capability building, and fostering a culture of responsible practice and continuous improvement. Citizenship and Service:  Contribute to leadership, governance, and service across the MD program by engaging in committees, partnerships, and administrative responsibilities while upholding university values and an inclusive culture.     This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .      About You    You are a collaborative and forward-thinking clinician educator with a passion for shaping the next generation of medical professionals. Bringing a strong foundation in clinical skills teaching and curriculum design, you are motivated by innovation in medical education and committed to fostering an engaging, high-quality learning experience for students. You thrive in a team-oriented academic environment, where your leadership, communication, and ability to influence positive change can make a meaningful impact. You will also have: Registration as a medical practitioner in Australia with an active clinical practice combined with demonstrated expertise in teaching and assessing core clinical skills. Strong experience in curriculum design, development, and alignment with learning outcomes, including the integration of clinical skills across the program. Proven ability to design and implement effective, evidence-based assessment strategies that support student learning and progression. Well-developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with academic, clinical, and professional colleagues. Demonstrated leadership capability, including mentoring educators, supporting team development, and contributing to a positive and inclusive culture. A commitment to educational excellence, continuous improvement, and the application of contemporary pedagogical approaches within medical education. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.     Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Wendy Findlay | Director, MD Curriculum and Assessment at w.findlay@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.       Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration (e.g. AHPRA), criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close 29th of September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68749).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for early to mid-October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Data-Science_R-68823": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Data Science",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFrazer Institute Full-time, fixed-term position until December 2028   Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A)  Based at our  Dutton Park Campus  About This Opportunity    Join a visionary and collaborative research team driving world-leading advances in melanoma and skin cancer research. Working at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, medical imaging, and clinical research, you will play a pivotal role in transforming complex research data into meaningful discoveries that have the potential to improve health outcomes globally. As a highly skilled Data Scientist, you will work alongside leading researchers, clinicians, AI specialists, and technical experts, leveraging cutting-edge technologies and world-class research infrastructure to develop innovative analytical solutions, enhance research capability, and help create lasting impact through data-driven discovery. At UQ, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while contributing to research that is part of something bigger, creating change together. Key responsibilities will include :    Research:  Design, develop and optimise clinical and skin imaging data management systems, workflows and research infrastructure. Apply advanced statistical, machine learning and data engineering methodologies to large-scale, longitudinal datasets, contributing to innovative melanoma and skin cancer research outcomes. Research Data Management and Analysis:  Lead the processing, cleaning, integration, validation and analysis of complex clinical and skin imaging datasets, including 2D and 3D medical imaging data. Develop, maintain and deploy robust, version-controlled analytical codebases and data solutions that support high-quality, reproducible research. Collaboration and Stakeholder Engagement:  Collaborate with researchers, clinicians, AI specialists and technical experts to enhance data platforms, modelling capability and analytical approaches. Provide specialist technical expertise, contribute to multidisciplinary research projects, and support continuous improvement across research systems and processes. Supervision and Researcher Development:  Train, mentor and support junior staff in data cleaning, quality assurance, data management and analytical best practices. Foster capability development and contribute to a collaborative and high-performing research environment. This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You    Completion of a PhD in Data Science, Biostatistics, Computer Science, Health Informatics, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated experience working with large, complex and longitudinal research datasets, including data cleaning, preprocessing, integration and quality assurance to support high-quality research outcomes. Proven expertise developing, validating and deploying advanced statistical and machine learning models using Python, R, or similar programming languages in a research environment. Experience working with melanoma, skin cancer or medical imaging research, including the analysis and management of 2D and 3D imaging datasets. Strong technical knowledge across the data lifecycle, including research data architecture, data pipelines, database management, SQL, REDCap, cloud or high-performance computing environments, and reproducible research practices. Excellent communication, stakeholder engagement and collaboration skills, with the ability to work effectively across multidisciplinary teams and provide training, mentoring and technical guidance to researchers and staff. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Caitlin Horsham at c.horsham@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.       Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.    Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in  Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close 14th of October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68823).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Nonequilibrium-Dynamics-in-Quantum-Computing_R-68679-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Nonequilibrium Dynamics in Quantum Computing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our vibrant and picturesque St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join a growing, collaborative research team developing innovative quantum algorithms for decarbonisation applications. Working within the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology and the Queensland Quantum Decarbonisation Alliance, your research will focus on dissipative state preparation for molecular systems and molecular dynamics. You will contribute to advancements in the use of nonequilibrium dynamics in quantum algorithms for applications in decarbonisation. Positioned at the intersection of fundamental discovery and real-world impact, the role offers opportunities to collaborate with quantum computing companies, application-domain partners, Queensland research institutions, and established experts in computational molecular and materials science and nonequilibrium statistical mechanics. Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Develop a coherent, quality research program focused on nonequilibrium dynamics, dissipative state preparation, and quantum algorithms for molecular systems, producing quality research outputs in high-quality outlets; Work with academic and industry partners to develop joint projects, pursue competitive research funding, and progress the translation of knowledge, technology, and practices to research end users. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Undergraduate Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, including feedback, coaching, and professional development. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through purposeful collaboration, participation in priority activities, support for academic colleagues and unit operations, and the development of relationships with industry, government, professional bodies, and the wider community. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in physics, mathematics, chemistry, computer science, engineering, or a closely related field. Strong foundational knowledge of, and research experience in, dissipative quantum dynamics. An emerging research profile in the dynamics of open quantum systems, quantum computing, and/or resource analysis of algorithms for fault-tolerant quantum computers. Experience in nonequilibrium statistical mechanics, molecular modelling, and/or quantum thermodynamics would also be beneficial. Evidence of quality publications in reputable refereed journals and experience presenting at conferences. Evidence of contributing to applications for external research funding or other mechanisms of research support. The capacity to collaborate effectively across disciplines and engage constructively with academic, government, and industry partners. The ability to contribute to student supervision, researcher development, internal service, and external engagement activities. About Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) The Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) creates solutions for some of society’s most serious problems in health, energy and the bioeconomy. Urgent solutions are needed for the way our global community generates and uses energy, engages with our environment, and deals with serious health issues. At the AIBN we're working on the answers. Our 400-strong team works closely with industry to translate cutting-edge science into practical solutions to help shift global reliance on fossil fuels; create healthier communities; build stronger, more sustainable economies; and create a more sustainable footprint overall. About Queensland Quantum Decarbonisation Alliance (QDA) The QDA brings together twenty-seven partners across academia and industry to create a world-leading decarbonisation effort in Queensland powered by quantum technologies. Led by UQ, it has the partnerships needed for success, including Griffith University, CSIRO and a range of industry partners including Siemens, Aurizon, Queensland Energy, among others. A key partner in the alliance is PsiQuantum, a leading quantum computing company and their decarbonisation affiliate Qlimate. PsiQuantum is currently building a utility-scale quantum computer in Brisbane. The QDA aims to pioneer the use of quantum computers for high-value decarbonisation projects such as improved battery chemistries, the development of new catalysts for hydrogen, biofuel or ammonia production, materials for hydrogen or carbon storage, or process optimisation in carbon-intensive industry. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Stephen Sanderson -   stephen.sanderson@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close 16 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68679)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Back-of-House-Nurse-Coordinator_R-68286-1": {
      "title": "Back of House Nurse Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUQ VETS . Full-time, permanent position. Base salary will be in the range $85,452 to $94,984 + 17% super (HEW Level 5). Join our Gatton Campus community and take a virtual tour . About This Opportunity Join the Veterinary Teaching Hospital in a purposeful coordination role that helps deliver outstanding patient care while supporting the development of the next generation of veterinary and veterinary technology professionals. Working in a collaborative clinical environment, you will coordinate daily service activities, provide high-level support to veterinary staff and clinical academics, and supervise and mentor veterinary nurses, technicians and nurse interns. Your leadership, clinical expertise and commitment to operational excellence will help create a positive experience for patients, clients, students and colleagues. Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Lead, mentor and develop veterinary nursing, technician and student teams, fostering clinical excellence, professional development and continuous improvement. Coordinate the efficient day-to-day operation of surgical, anaesthesia, intensive care and hospital services, including rostering, staff allocation, skill mix and workflow management. Oversee patient care across anaesthesia, surgery, recovery, intensive care, isolation and hospital wards, maintaining high standards of patient safety, treatment and husbandry. Coordinate surgical lists and respond to changing clinical priorities, including theatre turnovers, case re-ordering, staffing reallocations and urgent or emergency procedures. Act as the key floor coordinator and communication link between nursing, veterinary, specialist, emergency, client care and support teams, ensuring effective handover and resource coordination. Support clinical teaching, onboarding, performance management and continuing professional development for nurses, technicians, interns and students. Maintain and improve clinical protocols and SOPs, including anaesthesia, pain management, infection control, sterile technique and incident reporting, escalating safety concerns where required. Manage surgical and anaesthesia equipment, consumables and inventory, coordinating procurement and maintaining appropriate stock levels. Contribute to operational planning and service development, including staffing, equipment, workflow improvements and hospital leadership initiatives. About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders. Instead of solely concentrating on meeting caseload benchmarks or revenue targets, your priorities lie in creating an impact, establishing a lasting impression on the field, and conducting work that brings about positive changes in the lives of animals. The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Specialist Hospital is a largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest in veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a seven-day urgent and emergency care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as: Free parking and an intercampus bus . On-campus childcare , a medical clinic and a post office. Up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave or paid primary care leave. 17% super contributions and 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts, including Fitness Passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. A 25% discount on eligible UQ VETS services . Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sport facility . About You A Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, Bachelor of Applied Science in Veterinary Technology, Bachelor of Veterinary Technology or an equivalent qualification. Demonstrated senior veterinary nursing or veterinary technician experience within a large hospital or clinical environment, with the ability to deliver and maintain high professional standards across diverse areas of nursing. Demonstrated ability to supervise, mentor and support staff, including the capacity to build confidence, capability and collaborative working relationships. Strong written and verbal communication skills, high-level organisational capability, and experience managing professional and operational relationships with colleagues, clients and students. A genuine interest in supporting the clinical education of veterinary and veterinary technology students, together with a commitment to ongoing professional development and excellence. Demonstrated ability to work independently, exercise initiative and autonomy, respond flexibly to changing clinical priorities, and contribute effectively within a multidisciplinary team. Knowledge of relevant ethical requirements, policies and procedures, including workplace health and safety, equity and anti-discrimination obligations. A current radiation licence or the ability to apply for one. A postgraduate qualification in veterinary nursing or a relevant specialist qualification would be highly regarded. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sophie Alonso , s.alonso@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 11 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68286)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Rockhampton-Hospital/Senior-Administration-and-Engagement-Officer---RRM_R-69256": {
      "title": "Senior Administration and Engagement Officer - RRM",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nRural Clinical School Part-time 0.6FTE, permanent position Base salary will be in the range $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at the Rockhampton Hospital, however, could be supported in Bundaberg or Toowoomba   About This Opportunity    Are you passionate about creating meaningful connections and inspiring the next generation of rural medical professionals? Join the Rural Clinical School in a pivotal role that brings people, communities and opportunities together. As the Senior Administration and Engagement Officer, you will lead a diverse portfolio of engagement initiatives that support prospective students, current medical students and junior doctors, helping to strengthen rural medical pathways and promote rewarding careers in rural and remote healthcare. Working within a collaborative and purpose-driven team, you will play a key role in delivering exceptional experiences, fostering lasting stakeholder relationships and contributing to positive outcomes for rural communities through education and engagement. Key responsibilities will include: Coordinating innovative events, programs and engagement activities that inspire future students, support current learners and encourage junior doctors to pursue rural and remote medical careers. Building and nurturing strong relationships with students, alumni, volunteers, healthcare partners and community stakeholders to enhance participation, connection and the student experience. Developing clear communication and promotional materials, maintaining engagement databases and reporting on program outcomes to inform continuous improvement and future opportunities. Providing high-level coordination and administrative support to committees, meetings and working groups, ensuring effective governance, collaboration and delivery of strategic objectives. About You    Relevant tertiary qualifications and demonstrated experience in administration, stakeholder engagement, event management, communications, or a related field. Proven ability to build and maintain productive relationships with diverse stakeholders, with a strong customer service and engagement focus. Experience coordinating and delivering events, programs or initiatives, with excellent organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including experience developing communications, promotional materials and reports. Demonstrated ability to collect, maintain and analyse data to support program delivery, evaluation and continuous improvement. Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite and administrative systems, with experience using event management platforms and working effectively within a large or complex organisation. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Clare Butters, Senior Manager, Rural Clinical School at c.butters@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Thursday 24 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69256).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week of the 28th of September.   Applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Head-of-School--School-of-Mechanical-and-Mining-Engineering_R-65984-2": {
      "title": "Head of School, School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Mechanical and Mining Engineering Full-time, 5-year fixed-term position at Academic Level E, with underlying continuing (permanent) Professorial position Based at our beautiful  St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity Lead one of the world's most respected engineering schools and help shape the future of engineering education, research and industry impact. The School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering is entering an exciting new chapter. With a strong global reputation, significant student growth, world-leading research capability, and deep industry connections, the School is uniquely positioned to influence the future of mechanical, aerospace, biomedical, materials, mechatronic and mining engineering. As Head of School, you will inherit a thriving, collaborative and inclusive community and be empowered to drive its next phase of growth. This is a rare opportunity to build on an extraordinary legacy while shaping the ideas, partnerships and people that will solve tomorrow's most complex challenges. The School’s commitment to research excellence and real-world impact is reflected in the outstanding global standing of UQ's Mineral and Mining Engineering discipline, ranked 5th in the world in the 2026 QS World University Rankings by Subject . In this role, you will: Shape the strategic direction of one of Australia's leading engineering schools. Lead a community of internationally recognised researchers, educators, professional staff and industry collaborators. Drive research excellence across areas transforming the future, including advanced manufacturing, renewable energy, robotics, automation, biomedical engineering and mining innovation. Build bold interdisciplinary partnerships that translate discovery into meaningful impact. Strengthen engagement with industry, government and global partners to create new opportunities for growth, influence and collaboration. Elevate the School's global reputation while protecting the collegial, supportive culture that makes it distinctive. Guide strategic investment decisions across people, infrastructure, education and research. Leave a lasting legacy through leadership that creates impact well beyond the University. For more information about this opportunity, please view the Appointment Booklet. About You We are seeking a respected academic leader with an international reputation for excellence and a passion for shaping the future of research, education and industry impact. Combining intellectual leadership with strategic vision, you will bring the credibility to influence at the highest levels and the ability to unite people around a shared ambition. Our ideal candidate will demonstrate: An internationally recognised reputation for academic excellence , with the credibility, leadership capability and strategic vision to enhance the standing and influence of the School and Faculty. A proven track record of leading and developing high-performing teams , creating a culture of collaboration, innovation and excellence, and inspiring others to achieve ambitious goals in research, teaching and engagement. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, influencing and relationship-building skills , with the ability to forge productive partnerships across industry, government, professional bodies and the broader community. Strong strategic, operational and commercial acumen , including experience leading complex organisations and managing financial, human and physical resources to deliver sustainable growth and impact. A commitment to inclusive leadership and organisational stewardship , demonstrated through fostering equity, diversity and inclusion, supporting staff development and wellbeing, and navigating governance, policy and regulatory responsibilities with integrity. Most importantly, you are excited by the opportunity to lead a community with extraordinary potential and help define its next chapter. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. At the core of our teaching remains our students, and their experience with us sets a foundation for success far beyond graduation. UQ has made a commitment to making education opportunities available for all Queenslanders, regardless of personal, financial, or geographical barriers. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. Interested? If you'd like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Caylee-Jade Eve, Principal Consultant, Executive Recruitment via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au .  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section in the attached position booklet . Other Information Appointment to this role is subject to the successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, which will include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package. You can find out more about life in  Australia’s Sunshine State here .  We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We're also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday the 26th October at 11.00pm AEST ( R-65984 )."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow---Nutrition-and-Dietetics_R-67941-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow – Nutrition and Dietetics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Human Movement and Nutrition Sciences Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to two years Base salary will be in the range $119,462 to $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and inclusive academic community advancing public health nutrition through purposeful research, teaching and engagement. Working alongside academics in the School of Human Movement and Nutrition Sciences, you will develop an independent research profile, contribute to nationally and internationally recognised research, engage with policy and practice stakeholders, and translate evidence into meaningful outcomes for communities. Key responsibilities will include. Research: Develop an independent and team-based research program in public health nutrition, including nutritional epidemiology, population-based dietary interventions, nutrition promotion, and food and nutrition policy. Publish high-quality research, pursue external funding, apply rigorous quantitative and qualitative methods, and translate evidence to inform policy and practice. Teaching: Develop and deliver undergraduate and postgraduate teaching across different settings, contribute to innovative curriculum design, coordinate courses, and undertake assessment and marking in accordance with University requirements. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Master of Dietetics Studies research project students, Honours students, and Higher Degree by Research candidates. Support researcher capability, positive working relationships, and effective employee processes in accordance with University policies. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships with colleagues, government, industry, professional bodies, and the wider community. Contribute to governance, committees and internal service, while demonstrating leadership through mentoring, collaboration, and the consistent application of UQ values. This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion of a PhD in nutrition, dietetics, public health, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated research experience in population nutrition, behavioural nutrition, nutritional epidemiology, food policy, or another area of public health nutrition. A strong track record of publications in respected peer-reviewed journals and presentations at conferences, relative to career stage. Evidence of national standing and an emerging international profile in public health nutrition. Experience applying quantitative research methods to large datasets using advanced statistical methods and software such as R, Stata, or SAS. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining, or managing external research funding. Experience engaging effectively with government, community, policy, or practice stakeholders and working collaboratively within interdisciplinary teams. A growing record of student supervision, high-quality university teaching, academic service, and external engagement, supported by excellent communication, organisational, and project management skills. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. At UQ, you will be part of a visionary and connected research community committed to discovery, translation, and impact. You will be supported to bring together diverse perspectives, challenge conventional thinking, and create meaningful change in nutrition policy, practice, and population health. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Sarah McNaughton at s.mcnaughton@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 15 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67941)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Architectural-Technology_R-68599-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Architectural Technology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Architecture, Design and Planning – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years commencing January 2027 Base salary will be in the range $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Visa sponsorship may be available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  We are seeking a passionate and experienced Senior Lecturer in Architectural Technology to join our vibrant academic community at the School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) at our beautiful St Lucia campus. Shape the future of architecture and digital design at UQ and be part of a university consistently ranked in the world’s top 40. This exciting new role combines industry expertise with teaching, research and collaboration to inspire future design leaders and drive innovation. You will play a key role in advancing architectural technology, digital design, sustainability and AI across UQ's teaching and research programs. Collaborating with colleagues and industry, you will help equip graduates to tackle future built environment challenges. We welcome applicants who may prefer a different service fraction—please indicate your preferred arrangement in your cover letter. Flexible options, including job share, may be considered to accommodate the right candidate. If you are ready to make your mark in a world-class institution that invests in its people and their potential, we would love to hear from you. Key responsibilities will include:  Inspire through teaching – Shape the future of architecture with engaging, innovative courses, lectures and tutorials that connect theory to real-world practice, particularly in mainstreams of architectural technology and architectural science. This is expected to happen through leading design studios that connect deep architectural theory with computation, fabrication, performance and material experimentation. Lead impactful research – Build a strong research profile, publish in leading international journals, and secure funding to drive change across the built environment sector and in collaboration with other sectors/disciplines. Mentor future leaders – Guide and support Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to thrive as the next generation of architectural designers/scientists and design innovators. Engage and connect – Collaborate with colleagues, industry, government and the wider community while contributing to UQ’s vibrant academic culture. Drive technological innovation – Advance the School's strategic priorities in architectural technology by developing and integrating digital design methodologies, computational design, building performance analysis, digital fabrication, AI-assisted design, sustainable construction, and emerging technologies into teaching, research and industry engagement. In doing so, the successful candidate is expected to develop and critically evaluate bespoke AI and computational tools, rather than only the use of existing platforms. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  A PhD in Architecture, Architectural Science, Architectural Technology, or a related discipline. An established profile and leadership in teaching and research in architecture, architectural technology or architectural science.  Evidence of reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences.  Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing internal and external research funding.  Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups/classrooms.  Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area.  Technologically proficient and demonstrated leadership in integrating relevant digital and technical methods into curriculum, including critique of bias, authorship, automation and contextual relevance. Technically skilled and experienced, with a strong understanding of construction materials, systems and processes and their impact on documentation and detailing. An evidenced record of supervision of Honours and Research Higher Degree students to successful completion.  Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.  We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ali Cheshmehzangi (Head of School) at a.chesh@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68599)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer-in-Architectural-Design--T-R-_R-68595": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Architectural Design (T&R)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Architecture, Design and Planning – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years commencing January 2027 Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  This is a great opportunity to join the School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) as a Lecturer in Architectural Design , bringing expertise in Architectural Technology and Architectural Science. This newly created role offers the opportunity to shape the future of architectural design at a world top 40 university. Join a collaborative and supportive environment where teaching, research, and industry engagement thrive, with access to leading facilities and opportunities to inspire the next generation of design professionals. As the built environment evolves, this role will advance UQ’s teaching and research in architectural technology and design. You will contribute with your expertise in digital and computational design, BIM, sustainability, fabrication technologies, and AI, while collaborating across disciplines and industry to prepare graduates for future challenges in the built environment. We welcome applicants who may prefer a different service fraction—please indicate your preferred arrangement in your cover letter. Flexible options, including job share, may be considered to accommodate the right candidate. If you are ready to make your mark in a world-class institution that invests in its people and their potential, we would love to hear from you. Key responsibilities will include:  Inspire through teaching – Shape the future of architecture with engaging, innovative courses, lectures and tutorials that connect theory to real-world practice, particularly in mainstreams of architectural technology, architectural science, and tectonics. This helps connecting spatial theory directly with computational methods to ensure technology supports critical design thinking rather than a separate software stream. Lead impactful research – Build a strong research profile, publish in leading international journals, and secure funding to drive change across the built environment sector and in collaboration with other sectors/disciplines. Mentor future leaders – Guide and support Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to thrive as the next generation of architectural designers/scientists and design innovators. Engage and connect – Collaborate with colleagues, industry, government and the wider community while contributing to UQ’s vibrant academic culture. Drive technological innovation – Support the School's strategic priorities in architectural technology by integrating digital design methodologies, computational design, building performance analysis, architectural tectonics, AI-assisted design, sustainable construction, and emerging technologies into teaching, research and industry engagement. This also includes human-led critique and validation of AI outputs, including bias, authorship, copyright, automation and local contextual relevance. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  A PhD in Architecture/Architectural Design, Architectural Science, Architectural Technology, or a related discipline. A growing profile in teaching and research in architecture, architectural technology or architectural science/tectonics.  A developing reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences.  Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing internal and external research funding.  Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups/classrooms.  Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area.  Evidence of capability in computational and generative design, digital fabrication and building-performance tools, including critical and ethical teaching practice. Technically skilled and experienced, with a strong understanding of construction materials, systems and processes and their impact on documentation and detailing A growing record of supervision of Honours and Research Higher Degree students to successful completion.  Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.  We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ali Cheshmehzangi (Head of School) at a.chesh@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68595)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Associate-Director--Space_R-68800-1": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Space",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nAbout the Organisation   Capstone Recruitment is partnering with The University of Queensland (UQ) to appoint an experienced Associate Director, Space to join its Campus Planning team .   The University of Queensland is one of Australia’s leading research and education institutions, with a significant property portfolio supporting a diverse university community across multiple campuses.   UQ is embarking on an ambitious five-year, $500 million capital works program, comprising 57 critical projects across the estate. A key component of this program is the Space Strategy 2026–2031, which will transform how space is planned, allocated and utilised across the University.   To support this program, UQ is establishing a coordinated University-wide decant strategy to facilitate major works while maintaining operational continuity.   About the Role   Reporting to the Director, Campus Planning, the Associate Director, Space will provide strategic leadership and expert advice on the University's space requirements, utilisation and allocation.   A key focus of the role will be to lead the development and delivery of a comprehensive decant strategy supporting the University's capital works program. This will involve identifying and preparing suitable decant space, sequencing relocations and re-occupations, coordinating enabling works and ensuring operational continuity across a complex portfolio of projects.   The role will also lead the University's broader space management function, using data, systems and stakeholder engagement to optimise the utilisation of space and support long-term planning.   Key responsibilities include: Lead the University's strategic space planning, management and utilisation initiatives. Develop and deliver a University-wide decant strategy across multiple interdependent projects. Identify suitable decant solutions and coordinate enabling works and relocations. Provide strategic advice and reporting to senior executives and governance committees. Lead, coach and support a small specialist Space Management team. Build strong relationships across Faculties, Institutes, Schools and professional units.   This is a highly visible role with the opportunity to influence how one of Australia's largest and most complex institutional property portfolios is planned and utilised.   About You We are seeking an experienced property, space planning, facilities or program management professional who can operate strategically while managing complex, interdependent programs of work.   You will bring: Extensive experience managing complex programs, ideally within a large institutional environment. Demonstrated experience in space management, strategic planning or portfolio optimisation. Strong stakeholder management, communication and negotiation skills, with the ability to influence senior stakeholders and navigate competing priorities. Experience developing and implementing improvements to space management policies, systems and processes. Demonstrated leadership experience, including mentoring and developing teams. Experience with Archibus or a similar integrated facilities management / space management platform would be advantageous.   You will be a confident and collaborative leader who can bring structure to complex problems, communicate effectively with a diverse stakeholder group and drive practical outcomes across a large organisation.   To apply, please submit your resume or contact Capstone Recruitment for a confidential discussion about the opportunity.   Adrian de la Nougerede | 0401403101 | adrian@capstonerecruitment.com.au"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Awards-Officer_R-69264": {
      "title": "Senior Awards Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nResearch Office Full-time (100%), continuing position. Take ownership of complex research funding administration, provide specialist advice on contracts and compliance, and help researchers deliver impactful research outcomes. Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Based at our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus , with occasional travel to other UQ campuses. About This Opportunity The Research Office plays a pivotal role in supporting UQ's world-class research enterprise, partnering with researchers, faculties and professional teams to secure, establish and manage research funding that drives innovation and global impact. As a Senior Awards Officer, you will provide specialist administrative and advisory support across the post-award lifecycle of UQ’s research grants. Working in a fast paced and high volume environment, you will undertake detailed, hands on administration while managing more complex contractual, compliance and funding matters. A significant component of your day will involve establishing and managing research projects, coordinating grant documentation and agreements, processing project variations, monitoring reporting obligations, maintaining accurate records and progressing post-award workflow activities. Working closely with researchers, funding providers and professional staff across UQ, you will balance accuracy, responsiveness and sound judgement to support the timely activation and effective management of research projects. Key responsibilities will include: Managing a high volume of post-award administrative activities, including grant agreement processing, project establishment, project variations, reporting requirements, records management and workflow coordination. Preparing, reviewing, negotiating and coordinating research funding agreements and supporting documentation in accordance with University policies, procedures, risk requirements and funding provider conditions. Monitoring project milestones, contractual obligations and compliance requirements, following up outstanding information and ensuring records and systems remain accurate and current. Providing authoritative advice and resolving complex research funding, contractual and compliance matters through detailed analysis, sound judgement and practical problem solving. Liaising with researchers, funding providers and internal stakeholders to obtain documentation, progress matters and support timely research project activation and delivery. Mentoring and guiding team members, contributing to process improvement initiatives, policy and governance activities, quality assurance and cross functional projects. Please note restricted leave periods, intercampus travel and work outside standard hours during peak periods may be required. About You You are a highly organised and experienced research administration professional who is comfortable combining detailed administrative delivery with specialist advice and complex problem solving. You can maintain accuracy across a high volume of documentation and workflows, manage competing priorities and build productive relationships with a broad range of stakeholders. You will demonstrate: Relevant qualifications and considerable professional experience in research administration, grants administration, contract administration, compliance, governance or a related field, ideally gained within a higher education, research, government or similarly complex regulatory environment. Demonstrated experience managing the administration of research funding or similarly complex programs, including documentation, agreements, project establishment, variations, reporting, records and compliance workflows. Strong knowledge of relevant policies, procedures, funding rules and contractual requirements, with the ability to interpret complex information and provide clear, authoritative advice. Excellent organisational, analytical and problem solving skills, with demonstrated accuracy, attention to detail and the ability to manage high volumes of work and competing deadlines. Strong communication, negotiation and stakeholder engagement skills, together with experience supporting or mentoring team members and contributing to service and process improvements. Demonstrated project management capabilities, including the ability to manage competing priorities, exercise initiative and deliver outcomes within complex administrative environments. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside visionary and inquisitive minds from across the globe, united by a shared purpose to create meaningful change. At UQ, we are ambitious, collaborative and committed to excellence. You'll work alongside inquisitive and purpose-driven colleagues whose expertise contributes to outcomes that matter locally, nationally and globally. Join a community where contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. The broader benefits of joining UQ are extensive, including 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Maddison Taylor, Manager, Awards, via maddison.taylor1@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate transferable skills, relevant experience and their potential to succeed in the role. We’re also happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Applications close Monday 28 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69264). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for week commencing 5 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Hervey-Bay-Hospital/Deputy-Director--Rural-Clinical-School---Education_R-69003-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Director, Rural Clinical School - Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRural Clinical School Part-time (40%), fixed-term position until December 2028   Base salary will be in the range $175,404 - $193,070 + 17% super (Academic Level D)  Based at the Hervey Bay Hospital  About This Opportunity    Take a leading role in shaping the future of medical education across rural Queensland! As the academic leader responsible for the implementation and delivery of UQ’s innovative Doctor of Medicine (MD) Program within the Rural Clinical School, you will drive excellence in clinical education across a diverse network of rural and regional learning communities. Working alongside passionate clinicians, educators and professional staff, you will foster a collaborative and supportive academic environment where students thrive, develop their professional identity, and prepare to become the next generation of rural medical practitioners. This is a unique opportunity to create meaningful impact for communities across Queensland while contributing to one of Australia's most forward-thinking medical programs. Key responsibilities will include:    Teaching: Provide academic leadership and oversight for the implementation and delivery of the MD Program across the Rural Clinical School, drive excellence in curriculum and assessment design, contribute to clinical teaching and learner development, and support the training and development of clinical supervisors. Research: Maintain academic standing through research and the scholarship of teaching and learning, engage in research activities relevant to medical education, and foster a positive and collaborative research culture across the Rural Clinical School. Supervision and Researcher Development: Lead the responsible conduct of research, mentor and develop students and colleagues, provide coaching and constructive feedback, and support professional growth and high performance. Citizenship and Service: Collaborate with academic and professional colleagues to ensure successful delivery of the MD Program, contribute to governance and committee activities, promote rural medical careers, build strong stakeholder relationships, and demonstrate leadership through mentoring, collaboration and a commitment to staff wellbeing. This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You    Medical qualification with specialist registration (or eligibility for registration) with AHPRA and recognised clinical expertise in a general or specialist discipline. Postgraduate qualifications in medical education, health leadership, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of experience and expertise. Demonstrated success in leading and delivering high-quality medical education programs, including curriculum implementation, assessment and student support. Extensive experience in undergraduate, postgraduate and/or professional clinical teaching, with a commitment to educational excellence and innovation. Proven ability to build collaborative relationships and influence diverse stakeholders across academic, clinical and community settings. A genuine commitment to rural, regional and remote healthcare, with an understanding of the opportunities and challenges of medical education in these environments. About Rural Clinical School Located within the UQ Medical School, the Rural Clinical School (RCS) is the largest rural clinical school hosted by a single university. It works to foster and sustain a skilled and dynamic medical workforce to meet the needs of rural, regional, and remote Queensland. The Rural Clinical School provides students with rigorous clinical training built upon hands-on experiences in hospitals and community-based practices. Above all, we give our students the unique opportunity to live and work in some of Queensland’s amazing rural, regional, and remote locations. Our classrooms are the public and private hospitals, specialist consulting rooms, general practices and community healthcare settings throughout rural, regional and remote Queensland. Our teachers are dedicated and passionate rural clinicians. Our students get the opportunity to learn in a range of clinical contexts across the public, private and community sectors. The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ritta Partanen | Director, Rural Clinical School at r.partanen@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.    Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration (e.g. AHPRA), criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close 25 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69003).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for early October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Manager--International-Content-and-Communication_R-68503-2": {
      "title": "Manager, International Content and Communication",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFuture Students Division, Global Engagement Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to December 2027 with base salary in the range $120,317 - $134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8). Lead UQ's global content strategy and drive recruitment through audience focused communications. Manage a high performing team, internal and external stakeholder relationships, and global messaging frameworks for a top university. Based at  our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus   About This Opportunity Join a high performing, collaborative and purpose driven team responsible for connecting future international students with one of the world's leading universities. Reporting to the Senior Manager, International Marketing and Communication, you will lead UQ's international content and communication function, guiding a team of content and communication professionals to deliver audience centred, strategic and impactful content that support international student recruitment campaigns. This is a highly visible leadership opportunity where you will work together with the Manager, International Campaign and Prospect Engagement across a diverse portfolio spanning content, campaigns, social media, web, publications and events, while contributing to UQ's shared vision of creating change together. Key responsibilities will include: Leading the international content and communication function and contributing to the development and delivery of international marketing and communication strategies and annual work plans. Managing a team of content and communication professionals, ensuring priorities are delivered effectively and stakeholders receive exceptional support and advice. Driving the development of compelling international marketing collateral, communication and content that supports student recruitment objectives across global markets. Leading creative production, stakeholder relationships, stakeholder engagement, content governance, messaging frameworks and performance optimisation across multiple communication channels. About You You are a strategic communications leader who thrives in a collaborative environment and enjoys translating complex ideas into engaging content that resonates with diverse audiences. Combining strong leadership capability with exceptional communication skills, you will bring a proven ability to deliver impactful outcomes while building trusted relationships across a broad stakeholder landscape. Postgraduate qualifications and experience, or an equivalent combination of professional expertise, in communications, marketing, public relations, journalism or a related discipline, with experience leading high performing teams. Demonstrated success leading a communications and content function, including people leadership, budget oversight, strategic planning and the delivery of organisational priorities. Strong expertise across contemporary communication channels and content strategies, with a track record of producing high quality content and measurable communication outcomes for diverse audiences. Proven ability to manage complex creative projects and stakeholder relationships, including working effectively with external agencies, influencing decision makers and balancing competing priorities in a fast paced environment. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you'll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Colleen Stillman-Cheng at c.stillmancheng@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We're also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 11 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68503). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 19 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Program-Development-Analyst_R-69143-1": {
      "title": "Senior Program Development Analyst",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlanning and Business Intelligence (PBI) , based in the Office of the Provost Full-time (100%), permanent position with a high-performing, collaborative team Base salary will be in the range $120,317.28 - $134,892.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  The Planning and Business Intelligence (PBI) team at UQ have a rare opportunity for a highly organised, creative and analytical professional to join the team as a Senior Program Development Analyst. In this role, you will contribute to the development of innovative and relevant programs at UQ that respond to student, workforce and community priorities. Working within a small specialist team, you will collaborate with academic and professional stakeholders across the University to develop new program business cases and undertake the market analysis, program viability assessment and pricing analysis needed to inform decision-making. You will also contribute to PBI’s load planning, admissions management and Federal Government reporting functions. As PBI’s Senior Program Development Analyst, you will combine your natural curiosity, strategic thinking, analytical insight and sound judgement, with opportunities to strengthen governance and help shape new program development through evidence-based planning at UQ. Key responsibilities will include but are not limited to:  Lead the development of new program proposals by undertaking market, demand, viability and pricing analysis, and supporting the preparation of business cases to inform program approval decisions. Partner with academic and professional stakeholders across the University to guide program proposals through the Program Development Framework, providing strategic advice and facilitating informed decision-making. Design and deliver data analysis, scenario modelling, reports and insights that support program development, student load planning, admissions management and executive decision-making. Translate complex data and diverse stakeholder perspectives into clear recommendations, briefings and presentations for senior leaders and key stakeholders. Coordinate the annual tuition fee review process, undertaking market analysis and financial modelling to provide evidence-based pricing recommendations. Develop, implement and continuously improve governance and administrative processes that support strategic program development, program approval and student load planning activities. Lead the creation, curation and enhancement of data sources, datasets and reporting solutions, including identifying emerging data requirements and collaborating on data quality and system improvements. Contribute to the broader Student Load Planning & Program Development function through admissions monitoring, government reporting, ad hoc analysis and the provision of timely, accurate business intelligence. About You   You will have: A degree or postgraduate qualification with substantial relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. High-level analytical skills, including the ability to design analysis, apply rigorous logic to evaluate and interpret findings, apply sound business judgement to improve processes, provide sustainable solutions, and develop and present advice that supports strategic decision-making. High-level skills in compiling, manipulating and analysing complex administrative datasets using reporting tools such as SAP BusinessObjects (or similar) and Excel. Clear and succinct written and oral communication skills, including experience designing reports and/or dashboards to visualise and interpret data. Well-developed interpersonal skills, negotiation skills and demonstrated effectiveness in communicating with diverse audiences within a large and complex organisation. An excellent understanding of the environment and current issues in Australian higher education, particularly in relation to government policy, student demand, load monitoring and projection, and strategic issues in program development. Excellent organisational skills with a demonstrated ability to independently set goals, develop priorities, meet timelines, and to manage multiple and diverse tasks effectively. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Trudi Best - trudi.best@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 5 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69143). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be contacted and interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    }
  },
